hadiths[530]{hadithnumber,text}:
"9275","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Messenger ﷺ that when the verse When the help of Allah comes and the victory arrived, the Messenger ﷺ recited it to its completion and explained that mankind formed one camp while he and his Companions formed another camp, and that migration ended with the conquest while only jihad and intention remained. Marwan accused Abu Sa'id of lying in the presence of Rafi' ibn Khudayj (رضي الله عنه) and Zayd ibn Thabit (رضي الله عنه) as they sat with him on a couch, whereupon Abu Sa'id observed that fear of losing leadership and charity duties kept them silent until Marwan raised a stick to strike him and they then affirmed his truthfulness. > **""People are one camp, and I and my Companions are one camp. There is no hijrah after the conquest, but only jihad and intention.""**"
"9276","Mujashi' ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reports that he brought his nephew to the Prophet ﷺ to pledge allegiance for hijrah, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ declined that form and directed that the pledge be for Islam instead, since hijrah had ended after the conquest and the boy would belong among the righteous followers. > **""No, rather he shall pledge allegiance for Islam, for there is no hijrah after the conquest, and he shall be among the followers in goodness.""**"
"9277","Ghaziyyah ibn al-Harith (رضي الله عنه) reports that some youths of Quraysh intended to migrate to the Messenger ﷺ but their fathers forbade them, and when the matter was mentioned to the Messenger ﷺ he explained that hijrah had ended after the conquest and that only jihad and intention remained. > **""There is no hijrah after the conquest; there is only jihad and intention.""**"
"9278","Ghaziyyah (رضي الله عنه) reports in another narration that he heard the Prophet ﷺ state that hijrah had ended after the conquest and that only three matters remained, namely jihad, intention, and gathering. > **""There is no hijrah after the conquest; there are only three: jihad, intention, and gathering.""**"
"9279","Al-Harith ibn Ghaziyyah (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say on the day of the conquest of Makkah that hijrah had ended after the conquest and that only faith, intention, and gathering remained. > **""There is no hijrah after the conquest; there is only faith, intention, and gathering.""**"
"9280","Ibn al-Sa'di (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said hijrah would not cease while the enemy was fought. Mu'awiyah (رضي الله عنه), 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf (رضي الله عنه), and 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ described hijrah as two qualities, abandoning evil deeds and migrating to Allah and His Messenger, continuing while repentance is accepted until the sun rises from the west, after which every heart is sealed with its contents and people are spared further deeds. > **""Hijrah will not cease while the enemy is fought. Hijrah is two qualities: abandoning evil deeds, and migrating to Allah and His Messenger. Hijrah will not cease while repentance is accepted, and repentance will continue to be accepted until the sun rises from the west; when it rises, every heart will be sealed with what is in it and people will be relieved of deeds.""**"
"9281","Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah (رضي الله عنه) reports that some Companions of the Messenger ﷺ disagreed over whether hijrah had ceased, so he went to the Messenger ﷺ and asked about people claiming its cessation, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ affirmed that it would not cease while jihad continued. > **""Hijrah will not cease while jihad remains.""**"
"9282","'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'di (رضي الله عنه) of Banu Malik ibn Hanbal reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ with companions who asked him, as the youngest, to guard their mounts and then enter, after which he fulfilled their needs, sought permission, entered, and asked whether hijrah had ceased. The Prophet ﷺ replied that his need was better than theirs and that hijrah would not cease while the enemy was fought. > **""Your need is better than their needs. Hijrah will not cease while the enemy is fought.""**"
"9283","Raja' ibn Haywah reports from his father, from the envoy who asked the Messenger ﷺ about hijrah, that the Messenger ﷺ replied that it would not cease while the enemy was fought. > **""It will not cease while the enemy is fought.""**"
"9284","Thawban (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said hijrah would never cease while the disbelievers were fought. > **""Hijrah will never cease while the disbelievers are fought.""**"
"9285","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say there would be hijrah after hijrah toward the migration place of their father Ibrahim (peace be upon him), until only the worst people remained on earth, rejected by their land, detested by the mercy of the Most Merciful, gathered by fire with apes and swine, with the fire resting where they rest and lodging where they lodge, seizing whoever falls from them. > **""There shall be hijrah after hijrah to the migration place of your father Ibrahim, until none remains on earth except its worst people, whom their lands expel, whom the mercy of the Most Merciful detests, and whom the Fire shall gather with apes and swine, resting where they rest and lodging where they lodge, and whatever falls from them belongs to it.""**"
"9286","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa' (رضي الله عنه) reports that he came migrating to the Messenger ﷺ, who after prayer approached seated people rank by rank asking whether they needed anything until he reached him. When Wathilah sought Islam and hijrah, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he wanted desert hijrah or decisive hijrah, explained that the decisive form meant remaining with the Messenger ﷺ while the desert form meant returning to his land with obedience in hardship, ease, dislike, eagerness, and preference over him, then accepted his pledge with the allowance of ability. Wathilah later went to his family at Tabuk, greeted his father sitting in the sun with the greeting of Islam, and was answered with hope for goodness. > **""Do you have a need. What is your need. It is better for you. Will you migrate. The hijrah of the desert or the decisive hijrah. The decisive hijrah is that you remain with the Messenger of Allah, and the hijrah of the desert is that you return to your desert with hearing and obedience in your hardship and ease, dislike and eagerness, and preference over you, as far as you are able.""**"
"9287","Jubayr ibn Mut'im (رضي الله عنه) reports that he asked the Messenger ﷺ about claims that they had no reward in Makkah, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ assured him that their rewards would reach them even in a foxhole, then leaned toward him and confided that among his Companions were hypocrites. > **""Your rewards shall surely reach you even if you are in a foxhole. Among my Companions are hypocrites.""**"
"9288","Al-Farzdaq ibn Hibban reports that he and 'Ubaydullah ibn Haydah met 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As (رضي الله عنهما) on the Syrian road, who told of a bold Bedouin from their tribe asking the Messenger ﷺ whether hijrah was toward him wherever he was, toward a known land, toward a particular people, or ending with his death. The Messenger ﷺ fell silent briefly, called for the questioner, and declared that establishing prayer and paying zakah made one a migrant even if dying in Hadramah, a place in al-Yamamah. > **""Where is the questioner about hijrah. When you establish prayer and pay zakah, you are a migrant, even if you die in Hadramah.""**"
"9289","In another narration of the same encounter, the Messenger ﷺ defined hijrah as abandoning open and hidden immoralities, establishing prayer, and paying zakah, by which one becomes a migrant. > **""Hijrah is to abandon immoralities, what is apparent of them and what is hidden, and to establish prayer and pay zakah; then you are a migrant.""**"
"9290","Qays ibn Abi Hazim reports from Khalid ibn al-Walid (رضي الله عنه) that the Messenger ﷺ sent Khalid to some people of Khath'am who sought protection through prostration but were killed, so the Messenger ﷺ paid half their blood money and declared himself free of every Muslim residing among the polytheists, to the point that their fires should not see one another. > **""I am free of every Muslim who resides among the polytheists; their two fires shall not see one another.""**"
"9291","Ibn 'Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ, whenever he entered Makkah, supplicated Allah not to cause their deaths there until He brought them out from it. > **""O Allah, do not cause our deaths in it until You bring us out from it.""**"
"9292","Abu Musa (رضي الله عنه) reports that Sa'd (رضي الله عنه) fell ill in Makkah and the Prophet ﷺ visited him, whereupon Sa'd asked whether the Prophet disliked a man dying in the land from which he had migrated. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed this and supplicated that Allah elevate him, aid a people through him, and benefit others by him. > **""Yes. Perhaps Allah, blessed and exalted, will elevate you, aid a people through you, and benefit others by you.""**"
"9293","'Amr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jarhad reports hearing a man ask Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) who remained of the Prophet's Companions with him, to which Jabir named Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) and Salamah ibn al-Akwa' (رضي الله عنه). When the man claimed Salamah had apostatized from his hijrah, Jabir forbade that speech and recalled the Messenger ﷺ asking Aslam whether they would take up Bedouin life, and reassuring their fear of reverting after hijrah by affirming their migrant status wherever they were. > **""Will you take up Bedouin life, O Aslam. You are migrants wherever you are.""**"
"9294","Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' reports from his father that when Salamah (رضي الله عنه) arrived in Madinah, Buraydah ibn al-Husayb (رضي الله عنه) asked whether he had reverted from his hijrah. Salamah sought refuge in Allah, cited the Messenger's permission, and recalled the Messenger ﷺ inviting Aslam to Bedouin life, breathing breezes and dwelling in ravines, then reassuring their fear of harm to their hijrah by affirming their migrant status wherever they were. > **""Will you take up Bedouin life, O Aslam, breathing the winds and dwelling in the ravines. You are migrants wherever you are.""**"
"9295","Salamah ibn al-Akwa' (رضي الله عنه) reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who told him that they were his Bedouins while he was their townsman. > **""You are our Bedouins and we are your townsfolk.""**"
"9296","Muslim ibn Jarhad reports that Ibn 'Umar (رضي الله عنهما) fell ill and a man suggested buying goats and going to the green wilderness for health, whereupon Ibn 'Umar replied that none of them had been permitted to adopt Bedouin life except Aslam."
"9297","Shaddad (رضي الله عنه) reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, pledged for hijrah, then fell ill. When the Prophet ﷺ asked his condition, Shaddad explained that drinking from Bathana water would cure him but his hijrah prevented him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ ordered him to go and affirmed his migrant status wherever he was. > **""What is wrong with you, O Shaddad. What prevents you. Go, for you are a migrant wherever you are.""**"
"9298","'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn al-Atwal reports that his father 'Abdullah (رضي الله عنه) visited companions in Tustar, staying only the day of arrival and the next before leaving on the third, explaining from his father that the Messenger ﷺ had forbidden him from rural isolation, since settling in kharaj land amounted to such isolation, which he disliked. > **""The Messenger of Allah forbade me from desert seclusion; whoever settles in the land of kharaj has adopted seclusion, so I dislike remaining.""**"
"9299","Al-Bara' (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said whoever took up Bedouin life became harsh. > **""Whoever takes up Bedouin life becomes harsh.""**"
"9300","Jabir ibn Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ thrice cursed whoever adopted Bedouin life after hijrah, except in tribulation, when Bedouin life is better than remaining amid fitnah. > **""May Allah curse whoever takes up Bedouin life after hijrah. May Allah curse whoever takes up Bedouin life after hijrah. May Allah curse whoever takes up Bedouin life after hijrah, except in fitnah, for Bedouin life is better than residence during fitnah.""**"
"9301","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said the Muhajirun would have pulpits of gold on the Day of Resurrection, seated securely from terror, whereupon Abu Sa'id swore that had he favored anyone with that news he would have favored his own people. > **""The Muhajirun shall have pulpits of gold upon which they shall sit on the Day of Resurrection, safe from terror.""**"
"9302","'Imran ibn Hittan reports that 'A'ishah, Mother of the Believers (رضي الله عنها), asked him what they called villagers without lineage or kinship who joined them, and when he answered that they called them 'uluj and saqat, she replied that in the time of the Messenger ﷺ they used to call them Muhajirun. > **""We used to call them Muhajirun in the time of the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"9303","Salih ibn Bashir ibn Fudayk reports that Fudayk (رضي الله عنه) came to the Messenger ﷺ and asked about claims that whoever did not migrate perished, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ commanded prayer, zakah, abandoning evil, and residing wherever he wished in his people's land. > **""Establish prayer, pay zakah, abandon evil, and dwell in your people's land wherever you wish.""**"
"9304","Al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say that the earth belongs to Allah and His servants belong to Allah, so wherever one finds goodness he should fear Allah and settle. > **""The earth is the earth of Allah and the servants are the servants of Allah; wherever one of you finds goodness, let him fear Allah and settle.""**"
"9305","'Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) said that when three are on a journey they should appoint one of them over themselves, for that is a commander appointed by the Messenger ﷺ."
"9306","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said that when travelling, the most Quran-knowing among them should lead them even if youngest, and when he leads them he is their commander. > **""When you travel, let the most Quran-knowing among you lead you, even if he is the youngest among you; when he leads you, he is your commander.""**"
"9307","Ibn 'Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that when three are together, two should not converse privately excluding the third, and when three are travelling they should appoint one of them as leader. > **""When they are three, two should not converse privately excluding the third, and when three are on a journey, let them appoint one of them.""**"
"9308","'Abdullah (رضي الله عنه) said that when three are on a journey they should appoint one of them over themselves, and two should not converse privately excluding their companion."
"9309","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said that when one of them intends travel he should bid peace upon his brothers, for they add goodness to his supplication through their supplication. > **""When one of you intends travel, let him bid peace upon his brothers, for they add goodness through their supplication to his supplication.""**"
"9310","Safinah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ forbade travelling with the Quran to enemy land for fear the enemy would seize it. > **""The Messenger of Allah forbade travelling with the Quran to the land of the enemy, fearing the enemy would seize it.""**"
"9311","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ used to command them when campaigning that if a man at the rear called to the front to wait until he joined, they should wait for him. > **""O foremost, wait for him until he joins.""**"
"9312","Ibn 'Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that when the Messenger ﷺ appointed a commander over an army, he summoned him, enjoined Allah-consciousness and goodness toward accompanying Muslims, then commanded fighting in Allah's name against disbelievers without betrayal, treachery, mutilation, or killing children, and instructed inviting polytheist enemies to one of three courses: Islam and then hijrah with rights and duties of Muhajirun, or the status of Bedouin Muslims without share in fay' and ghanimah while subject to Allah's law, without granting Allah's judgment or protection as one's own but granting one's own protection, since violating one's own protection is lighter than violating Allah's protection. > **""Fight in the name of Allah; fight those who disbelieve in Allah; do not embezzle, betray, mutilate, or kill a child. When you meet your polytheist enemy, invite them to one of three courses: invite them to Islam and to hijrah, then invite them to the status of Bedouin Muslims; do not impose upon them the judgment of Allah, but impose your own judgment; do not grant them the protection of Allah, but grant them your protection and that of your companions.""**"
"9313","Ibn 'Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger ﷺ sent word to Mu'adh (رضي الله عنه) and Abu Musa (رضي الله عنه) to consult one another, obey one another, make ease without hardship, and give glad tidings without repulsion. > **""Consult one another, obey one another, make ease and do not make hardship, give glad tidings and do not repel.""**"
"9314","Ibn 'Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that in fertile land they should shorten pace and give mounts their due, for Allah is gentle and loves gentleness, while in barren land they should hasten through, keep to night travel since the earth is folded at night, and avoid encamping on the highway which shelters snakes and haunts beasts. > **""When the land is fertile, shorten the pace and give the mounts their due, for Allah is gentle and loves gentleness. When the land is barren, hasten through it. Keep to night travel, for the earth is folded at night. Beware of encamping on the highway, for it is the refuge of snakes and the resort of beasts.""**"
"9315","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said that when travelling in fertile land they should give animals their right or share, when in barren land they should hasten through, keep to night travel since the earth is folded at night, and avoid encamping on the highway which shelters every creature. > **""When you travel in fertile land, give the animals their due. When you travel in barren land, hasten through it. Keep to night travel, for the earth is folded at night. When you encamp, do not encamp on the highway, for it is the refuge of every creature.""**"
"9316","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ used to leave by Bab al-Shajarah and return by the road of al-Mu'arras."
"9317","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said Satan targets one and two travellers, but not three. > **""Satan targets one and two, but when they are three he does not target them.""**"
"9318","Aslam reports that after returning from travel, 'Umar (رضي الله عنه) asked whom he had accompanied, and when he named a man of Bakr ibn Wa'il, 'Umar asked whether he had not heard the Messenger ﷺ warn about trusting the Bakri brother. > **""Your brother is the Bakri, yet do not trust him.""**"
"9319","Ibn 'Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said the best companions are four, the best detachments four hundred, the best armies four thousand, and a people reaching twelve thousand are not defeated from fewness when truthful and patient. > **""The best companions are four, the best detachments are four hundred, the best armies are four thousand, and a people reaching twelve thousand shall not be defeated from fewness, when they are truthful and patient.""**"
"9320","Suwayd ibn Hubayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ, in one narration heard directly, that the best wealth for a man is a tractable filly or a grafted palm row. > **""The best wealth of a man for him is a tractable filly or a grafted palm row.""**"
"9321","Ma'qil ibn Yasar (رضي الله عنه) reports that nothing was dearer to the Messenger ﷺ than horses, then supplicated concerning camels and women. > **""Nothing was dearer to the Messenger of Allah than horses. O Allah, pardon concerning camels and women.""**"
"9322","Abu Sa'id (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that goodness is bound to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection. > **""Goodness is bound to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9323","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ addressed him to remember his words, that a young she-goat reaching a Muslim is better than Uhud in gold left behind, that the wealthy are the least on Resurrection Day except those who spend thus and thus, and that goodness lies in the forelocks of horses until Resurrection. > **""O Abu Dharr, remember what I say to you: a young she-goat reaching a Muslim man is better for him than Uhud in gold which he leaves behind. O Abu Dharr, remember what I say to you: the wealthy are the least on the Day of Resurrection except one who says thus and thus. Remember, O Abu Dharr, what I say to you: goodness is in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9324","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness is bound to the forelocks of horses until Resurrection, and the spender upon them is like one begging with charity. > **""Goodness is bound to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, and the spender upon them is like one soliciting with charity.""**"
"9325","Hudhayfah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said sheep are blessing, camels are honour for their people, goodness lies in horses' forelocks until Resurrection, and one's servant is one's brother to be treated well and aided when overpowered. > **""Sheep are blessing, camels are honour for their people, goodness is in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, and your servant is your brother, so treat him well and aid him if you find him overpowered.""**"
"9326","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until Resurrection. > **""Goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9327","Sawadah ibn al-Rabi' (رضي الله عنه) reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who granted him young camels and ordered him to tell his family to trim nails lest they injure udders of livestock, adding that goodness is bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection. > **""When you return to your family, command them to trim their nails lest they injure the udders of their livestock. Goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9328","Abu Kabshah, Companion of the Messenger ﷺ (رضي الله عنه), reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness is bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection, their owners are aided through them, and the spender is like one extending his hand with charity. > **""Goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, their owners are aided through them, and the spender upon them is like one extending his hand with charity.""**"
"9329","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness and blessing are bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection, their owners are aided, and they should be adorned but not with bowstrings. > **""Goodness and blessing are bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, their owners are aided through them; adorn them but do not adorn them with bowstrings.""**"
"9330","'Urayb (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that goodness and nobility are bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection, owners are aided, the spender is like one extending his hand in charity, and their urine and dung shall be as paradisal musk for their owners before Allah on Resurrection Day. > **""Goodness and nobility are bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, their owners are aided through them, the spender upon them is like one extending his hand in charity, and their urine and dung shall be for their owners before Allah on the Day of Resurrection as the musk of Paradise.""**"
"9331","Al-Nu'man (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until Resurrection. > **""Goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9332","Al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan asked Ibn al-Hanzaliyyah (رضي الله عنه) to narrate what he heard from the Messenger ﷺ, whereupon he recalled hearing that goodness is bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection with owners aided, and that spending on a horse kept in Allah's path is like extending the hand with charity without withholding. > **""Goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, their owners are aided through them, and whoever keeps a horse in the path of Allah, spending upon it is like one extending his hand with charity and not withholding it.""**"
"9333","Sawadah ibn Rabi' al-Jarmi (رضي الله عنه) reports that he came to the Messenger ﷺ, who granted him young camels and enjoined holding to horses, for goodness is bound in their forelocks until Resurrection. > **""Hold to horses, for goodness is bound in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9334","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ owned a horse which he gifted to an Ansari man whose neighing was heard until it ceased, and when asked the man said he had gelded it, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ declared that goodness and spoils lie in horses' forelocks until Resurrection, their forelocks their warmth and their tails their fly-whisks. > **""What happened to your horse. Goodness and spoils are in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection; their forelocks are their warmth and their tails are their fly-whisks.""**"
"9335","Khabbab ibn al-Aratt (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said horses are three: for the Most Merciful, for man, and for Satan. The horse of the Most Merciful is kept in Allah's path and fought upon against His enemies, the human horse is used for belly and burden, and the Satanic horse is used for racing and gambling. > **""Horses are three: a horse for the Most Merciful, a horse for man, and a horse for Satan. The horse of the Most Merciful is what is kept in the path of Allah and fought upon against the enemies of Allah. The horse of man is what is kept for belly and burden. The horse of Satan is what is raced upon and gambled upon.""**"
"9336","'Ali (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said whoever keeps a horse in Allah's path, its fodder and traces shall lie in his scale on Resurrection Day. > **""Whoever keeps a horse in the path of Allah, its fodder and its traces shall be in his scale on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9337","A man of the Ansar reports from the Prophet ﷺ that horses are three: one kept in Allah's path whose price, riding, lending, and fodder are reward; one kept for rivalry and betting whose price, riding, lending, and fodder are burden; and one for belly which may suffice against poverty if Allah wills. > **""Horses are three: a horse which a man keeps in the path of Allah, its price is reward, its riding is reward, its lending is reward, and its fodder is reward; a horse upon which a man contends and bets, its price is burden, its riding is burden, its lending is burden, and its fodder is burden; and a horse for belly which may prove a barrier against poverty, if Allah wills.""**"
"9338","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ that horses are three: for the Most Merciful, for man, and for Satan. The Merciful horse is kept in Allah's path with its fodder, urine, and dung recorded, the Satanic horse is gambled and raced upon, and the human horse is kept seeking its belly and serves as cover from poverty. > **""Horses are three: a horse for the Most Merciful, a horse for man, and a horse for Satan. The horse of the Most Merciful is what is kept in the path of Allah. The horse of Satan is what is gambled upon and raced upon. The horse of man is what a man keeps seeking its belly, and it is a cover from poverty.""**"
"9339","Asma' bint Yazid (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness is eternally bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection, so whoever keeps one prepared in Allah's path and spends upon it seeking reward, its satiety, quenching, thirst, dung, and urine become success in his scales, while whoever keeps it for show, repute, joy, and exultation finds its feeding, hunger, quenching, dung, and urine as loss in his scales. > **""Goodness is bound in the forelocks of horses forever until the Day of Resurrection. Whoever keeps it prepared in the path of Allah and spends upon it seeking reward in the path of Allah, its satiety, quenching, thirst, dung, and urine are success in his scales on the Day of Resurrection. Whoever keeps it for show, repute, joy, and exultation, its satiety, hunger, quenching, dung, and urine are loss in his scales on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9340","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said goodness and nobility are bound in horses' forelocks until Resurrection with owners aided, commanding wiping forelocks, supplicating blessing, adorning them but not with bowstrings, with 'Ali adding not to adorn them with idols. > **""Goodness and nobility are bound in the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection, their owners are aided through them, so wipe their forelocks, supplicate blessing for them, adorn them but do not adorn them with bowstrings.""**"
"9341","Sahl ibn Sa'd (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ had three horses kept with his father which his father fed, naming them al-Lazzaz, al-Luhayf, and al-Zarib."
"9342","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ had a horse which glided swiftly and pleased him, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ declared that his horse was a sea. > **""My horse of mine is but a sea.""**"
"9343","Ibn 'Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger ﷺ had a horse called al-Murtajiz."
"9344","Abu Wahb al-Kula'i (رضي الله عنه) was asked why he preferred the ashqar horse and explained that the Messenger ﷺ had sent a detachment whose first bearer of victory news rode an ashqar horse. > **""The Messenger of Allah sent a detachment, and the first to come with victory was the rider of the ashqar.""**"
"9345","Ibn 'Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said the blessing of horses lies in their dark red colour, and the most blessed forelock belongs to the blaze-faced, white-legged horse free on the right foreleg. > **""The blessing of horses is in their dark red colour, and the most blessed forelock is that of the blaze-faced, white-legged horse free on the right foreleg.""**"
"9346","'Uqbah ibn 'Amir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said that intending a campaign one should buy a blaze-faced, white-legged horse free on the right side, for then one remains safe and gains spoils. > **""When you intend to campaign, buy a blaze-faced, white-legged horse free on the right, for you shall remain safe and gain spoils.""**"
"9347","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ warn against feeble horses, for if they meet they flee and if they gain spoils they betray. > **""Beware of feeble horses, for if they meet they flee, and if they gain spoils they betray.""**"
"9348","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to discipline horses, for they accept discipline. > **""Discipline horses, for they accept discipline.""**"
"9349","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger ﷺ would sometimes twist the forelock of his horse with his hand."
"9350","Ju'ayl al-Ashja'i, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he went out on one of the campaigns with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, riding a lean and weak mare of his, so he fell to the rear of the people until the Messenger caught up with him and called out to him to ride forward as the owner of the horse. Ju'ayl replied that she was lean and weak, so the Messenger lifted a small whip he had with him, struck her with it, and supplicated for blessing in her for him. Ju'ayl said that thereafter he found himself holding back her head lest she outpace the people, and that he later sold twelve thousand worth from her offspring. > **""Move along, O owner of the horse. O Allah, bless him in her.""**"
"9351","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that contest with stakes is lawful only in the racing of camels, the racing of horses, or the shooting of arrows. > **""No contest with stakes is lawful except in the light-footed camel, the solid-hoofed horse, or the arrowhead.""**"
"9352","Abdullah ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to line up Abdullah, Ubaydullah and Kathir, the sons of al-Abbas, and promise a specified gift to whichever of them reached him first, whereupon they would race toward him and fall upon his back and chest, and he would kiss them and embrace them. > **""Whoever of you reaches me first shall have such-and-such.""**"
"9353","Kathir ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to gather him together with Abdullah, Ubaydullah and Qutham, then spread his arms wide and promise a specified gift to whichever of them reached him first. > **""Whoever of you reaches me first shall have such-and-such.""**"
"9354","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, held a race between horses, fixed a prize between them, set an entering third horse to legalize the stake, and stated that contest with stakes is lawful only in the horse race or in archery. > **""No contest with stakes is lawful except in the hoof or the arrowhead.""**"
"9355","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, held a race between horses and placed a wager upon it."
"9356","Abu Labid Limazah ibn Ziyad reported that horses were sent out for racing in the time of al-Hajjaj when al-Hakam ibn Ayyub was governor of Basrah, so the people went to the racecourse and then decided to visit Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, and ask him whether the Companions used to race for stakes in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and whether the Messenger himself raced. Anas replied in the affirmative and swore by Allah that the Messenger raced upon a horse called Subhah, which outpaced the people, and that this delighted him and pleased him."
"9357","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made the horses lean for racing and held a race between them, then saw Jabir riding a camel and told him that he was constantly straining it, meaning that he kept beating it. > **""O Jabir, you keep straining him.""**"
"9358","Urwah ibn Mudarris, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was driving his horse in front of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praised Allah for blessing in the shaping of their hooves and their lower parts. > **""Blessed is Allah Who fashioned their hooves and their lower legs.""**"
"9359","Ismah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mounted a horse and galloped upon it, then returned to the Companions and described its swiftness and ease of running to be like the sea in its breadth and flow. > **""We found him to be a sea.""**"
"9360","Abdullah ibn Mughaffal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that one day while the people were in Madinah, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to them upon a horse of his and rode off until he disappeared from their sight, then came back while it was galloping, whether he was urging it on or it had bolted with him. It passed by a tree from which a bird flew out, so the horse swerved and the Messenger fell from it onto rough ground. The Companions hastened to him and found him sitting with his knees, hip joints, shoulders and face scraped and the skin stripped off with yellowish fluid showing, so they sat around him weeping."
"9361","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made the horses lean for racing, fixed a time for their training, announced a specified day and a specified place for their race, and set a shorter course for the horses that had not been trained. > **""Such-and-such day at such-and-such place.""**"
"9362","Iyad al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, asked who would race him, whereupon a youth answered that he would if Abu Ubaydah would not become angry. The youth then outpaced him, and the narrator saw the two locks of Abu Ubaydah's hair bouncing as he rode behind the youth upon a bareback horse."
"9363","Abu Balj reported that he saw Lubayy ibn Laba al-Asadi, may Allah be pleased with him, who was among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and had met the Prophet. His horse had won the race, and he had covered it with an Adeni cloak, while he himself was wearing a garment of silk-mixed wool and an outer cloak with marks."
"9364","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever entices a slave against his master, whoever corrupts a wife against her husband, and whoever shouts and clamours at horses on the day of the race is not of the Muslims in conduct. > **""He is not of us who entices a slave against his master, nor is he of us who corrupts a wife against her husband, nor is he of us who clamours at horses on the day of the wager.""**"
"9365","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that there is no jalab in Islam, meaning the practice of shouting at or crowding racehorses or alms-camels to influence the contest or collection. > **""There is no jalab in Islam.""**"
"9366","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that there is no shighar in Islam, that is the exchange of sisters in marriage without dowry, and that there is no jalab and no janab, meaning shouting at racehorses or holding a spare horse aside in racing to gain advantage. > **""There is no shighar in Islam, nor any jalab nor any janab.""**"
"9367","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the castration of horses and other animals, and Ibn Umar added that in leaving them intact lies the growth of creation."
"9368","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sternly forbade tying up a living creature as a target and forbade the castration of animals with strong prohibition."
"9369","Dihyah al-Kalbi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, whether he should breed a donkey with a mare for him so that a mule would be produced for him to ride, whereupon the Messenger replied that only those who lack knowledge do such a thing. > **""Only those who have no knowledge do that.""**"
"9370","Abu Amir al-Hawzani reported that Abu Kabshah al-Anmari, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him and asked him to lend him his stallion for breeding, saying that he had heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that whoever lends his horse for breeding and a foal results from it shall have the reward of seventy horses given for riding in the path of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and in another wording that whoever lends his horse to a Muslim and a foal results shall have that reward, while if no foal results he shall still have the reward of a single horse given for riding in the path of Allah. > **""Whoever lends his stallion for breeding and a foal results for him shall have the reward of seventy horses mounted in the path of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and if no foal results he shall have the reward of a single horse mounted in the path of Allah.""**"
"9371","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that people never extend to one another anything more excellent than lending for breeding, for when a man lends his mare the reward continues for him, when a man lends his stallion the reward continues for him, and when a man lends his ram the reward continues for him."
"9372","Muhammad ibn Salam reported from some of his companions that Salman ibn Rabi'ah inspected horses and Amr ibn Ma'dikarib passed by upon his horse, whereupon Salman called it a hajin, meaning of mixed breed, and Amr insisted it was an atiq, meaning purebred. At Salman's order the horse was kept thirsty, then a trough of water was brought and the purebred horses were called and drank, then Amr's horse came, folded its forelegs and drank, which is the manner of the crossbred. Salman pointed this out to him, and Amr retorted that the crossbred recognizes the crossbred. When this reached Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he wrote to Amr rebuking him for what he had said to his commander and warning him by oath that though Amr named his sword al-Samsamah, Umar had a cutting sword which, if laid upon his head, would not be lifted until it reached what he mentioned of his insides, adding that if Amr wished to learn whether this was true he would do it."
"9373","Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave al-Zubayr one share of the spoils, gave his mother one share, and gave his horse two shares."
"9374","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, seated him behind himself upon the mount while Qutham was seated in front of him."
"9375","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported in a version with al-Bazzar that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, poured forth from Muzdalifah or from Arafah with Qutham seated in front of him and al-Fadl seated behind him."
"9376","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that the owner of the mount has the greatest right to its front seat. > **""The owner of the mount has the greatest right to its front.""**"
"9377","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that Allah has angels who descend every night and hold back dogs from the mounts of the warriors, except for a mount that has a bell upon its neck. > **""Indeed Allah has angels who descend every night and hold back dogs from the mounts of the warriors, except a mount that has a bell upon its neck.""**"
"9378","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that some people complained to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he supplicated for them and commanded them to practise nasalan, a brisk swinging walk, whereupon they walked briskly and found it lighter upon them. > **""You must practise nasalan.""**"
"9379","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that he was sent before the Hour with the sword until Allah alone is worshipped without partner, that his provision was placed under the shadow of his spear, and that disgrace and abasement were placed upon whoever opposes his command. > **""I was sent before the Hour with the sword until Allah, the Exalted, alone without partner is worshipped, and my provision was placed under the shadow of my spear, and disgrace and abasement were placed upon whoever opposes my command.""**"
"9380","Uwaym ibn Sa'idah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw a man carrying a Persian bow and ordered him to cast it aside, then pointed to the Arab bow and said that through it and the spears of qanah wood Allah would grant the Muslims mastery in the lands and victory over their enemy. > **""Cast it aside. Through this and the spears of qanah Allah will grant you mastery in the lands and victory over your enemy.""**"
"9381","Abdullah ibn Busr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, to Khaybar and wound a black turban upon him, letting its end hang behind him or, as the narrator doubted, upon his left shoulder. Then the Messenger went out following the army leaning upon a bow, and a man carrying a Persian bow passed by him, so he ordered him to cast it away, declaring it accursed and its bearer accursed, and commanded adherence to spears and Arab bows, for through them Allah would honour their religion and open the lands for them. Yahya ibn Hamzah added that the Messenger said this because of the conditions of that time, whereas today such bows have become equipment and strength for the people of Islam. > **""Cast it away, for it is accursed and whoever carries it is accursed. Hold to the spears and the Arab bows, for through them Allah will honour your religion and open the lands for you.""**"
"9382","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported in a raised narration that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded adherence to archery, for it is the best, or among the best, of pastimes, and in the wording of al-Tabarani that it is the best of games. > **""Hold to archery, for it is the best of your pastimes.""**"
"9383","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that the angels do not attend any of the contests with stakes except those of arrows and shooting at targets. > **""The angels do not attend any of your contests with stakes except those of arrows and shooting.""**"
"9384","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by a people who were shooting and told them to shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for their father was an archer. > **""Shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for your father was an archer.""**"
"9385","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed by a people who were shooting and told them to shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for their father was an archer. > **""Shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for your father was an archer.""**"
"9386","Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to the people of Aslam to shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for their father was an archer, and then said that he was with Mihjan ibn al-Adra', whereupon the people held back. When he asked them what was wrong and they replied that whoever he sided with would surely win, he told them to shoot and that he was with all of them. > **""Shoot, O sons of Isma'il, for your father was an archer. Shoot, and I am with all of you.""**"
"9387","Amr ibn Atiyyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that the land would soon be opened for the Muslims and worldly provision sufficed for them, so none of them should be too feeble to amuse himself with his arrows, meaning to keep up archery. > **""The land shall soon be opened for you and the world sufficed for you, so let none of you be too feeble to amuse himself with his arrows.""**"
"9388","A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that it is no fault upon a person, when worry presses upon him, to hang his bow upon himself and thereby dispel his worry. > **""It is no fault upon any of you, when worry presses upon him, to hang his bow upon himself and thereby dispel his worry.""**"
"9389","Qays ibn Abi Hazim reported that he saw Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of Yarmuk shooting between two targets with men from the Companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, alongside him, and that Khalid said they had been commanded to teach their children archery and the Quran."
"9390","Ata ibn Abi Rabah reported that he saw Jabir ibn Abdullah and Jabir ibn Ubaydullah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with them, shooting at targets until one of them grew weary and sat down, whereupon the other asked him whether he had grown lazy, saying that he had heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that everything not connected with the remembrance of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is pastime or heedlessness except four qualities: a man's walking between the two targets, his training of his horse, his sporting with his wife, and his teaching of swimming. > **""Everything not from the remembrance of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is pastime or heedlessness, except four qualities: a man's walking between the two targets, his training of his horse, his sporting with his wife, and his teaching of swimming.""**"
"9391","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that everything of worldly pastime is false except three: shooting with the bow, training the horse, and sporting with the wife, for these are of the truth. He further said to shoot and to ride, though shooting is dearer to him, and that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, admits three persons into Paradise for a single arrow: its maker who hopes for reward in it, its supplier, and its shooter. > **""Everything of the pastime of the world is false except three: your shooting with your bow, your training of your horse, and your sporting with your wife, for they are of the truth. Shoot and ride, and that you shoot is dearer to me. Indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, admits three into Paradise for a single arrow: its maker who hopes for reward therein, its supplier, and its shooter.""**"
"9392","Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that every pastime is disliked except a man's sporting with his wife, his walking between the two targets, and his training of his horse. > **""Every pastime is disliked except a man's sporting with his wife, his walking between the two targets, and his training of his horse.""**"
"9393","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever walks between the two archery targets shall have a good deed recorded for every step. > **""Whoever walks between the two targets shall have a good deed for every step.""**"
"9394","Mujahid reported that he saw Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, running vigorously between the two archery targets and saying that he sought reward through it, repeating words to that effect."
"9395","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever learns archery and then forgets it has denied a blessing. > **""Whoever learns archery and then forgets it, it is a blessing he has denied.""**"
"9396","Utbah ibn Abd al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to his Companions to stand and fight, whereupon a man shot an arrow and the Prophet declared that this man had thereby made Paradise obligatory for himself. > **""Stand and fight. This one has made it obligatory.""**"
"9397","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it falls short or reaches its mark, shall have reward equal to freeing four persons from the sons of Isma'il. > **""Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it falls short or reaches, shall have the like of the reward of one who freed four persons from the sons of Isma'il.""**"
"9398","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah shall have it as light for himself on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9399","Utbah ibn Abd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said on the day of Qurayzah and al-Nadir that whoever put an arrow into that fortress would have Paradise made obligatory for him, whereupon Utbah said that he put three arrows into it. > **""Whoever puts an arrow into this fortress, Paradise has become obligatory for him.""**"
"9400","Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah reported that he saw Abu Amr al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who had been present at Badr, al-Aqabah and Uhud, while he was fasting and writhing from thirst, telling his boy to hand him his quiver. The boy handed it to him, so he drew an arrow with a weak drawing and shot three arrows, then said that he had heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it falls short or reaches, shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection. The narrator added that Abu Amr was killed before sunset. > **""Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it falls short or reaches, shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9401","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that whoever grows a grey hair in Islam shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it misses or hits, shall have reward like freeing a slave from the descendants of Isma'il. > **""Whoever grows a grey hair in Islam shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it misses or hits, shall have the like of freeing a slave from the descendants of Isma'il.""**"
"9402","Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever grows a grey hair in Islam shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah shall have a degree recorded for him by Allah. > **""Whoever grows a grey hair in Islam shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, Allah shall record a degree for him thereby.""**"
"9403","Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that a man's standing in the rank in the path of Allah is better than the world and all it contains, that whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah and it reaches its mark, whether it misses or hits, earns the reward of freeing a slave, and that whoever grows grey hair in the path of Allah shall have it as light on the Day of Resurrection."
"9404","Thumamah reported that Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, used to sit with a mattress spread for him while his sons shot before him. One day he came out to the narrators while they were shooting and told them, O my sons, how poorly they were shooting, then took the bow himself and shot without missing the target."
"9405","Al-Qasim reported that the first to spread the Quran from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, the first to build a mosque in which prayer was offered was Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, the first to call the adhan was Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, the first whose horse carried him forth in the path of Allah was al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad, may Allah be pleased with him, the first to shoot an arrow in the path of Allah was Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, the first of the Muslims killed on the day of Badr was Mihja', the freed slave of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, the first tribe to make alliance with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was Juhaynah, and the first to pay charity willingly of their own accord were the Banu Udhrah ibn Sa'd."
"9406","Marzuq al-Saqil reported that he polished the sword of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, Dhu al-Faqar, which had a pommel of silver, rings upon its suspension, and a silver pulley-like ornament in its middle."
"9407","Utbah ibn Abd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asked to be shown his sword, so Utbah drew it and the Prophet looked at it and found thinness and weakness in it, whereupon he told him not to strike with it but to thrust with it. > **""Show me your sword. Do not strike with this, but thrust with it.""**"
"9408","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had a sword whose hilt and pommel-cap were of silver and which was called Dhu al-Faqar. He had a bow called al-Saddad, a quiver called al-Jam', a cuirass plated with brass called Dhat al-Fudul, a spear called al-Nab'a', a shield called al-Dafn, a white shield called al-Mujaz, a black horse called al-Sakb, a saddle called al-Dahh, a grey mule called al-Duldul, a she-camel called al-Qaswa, a donkey called Ya'fur, a mat called al-Karr, a small spear called al-Namir, a water vessel called al-Sadir, a mirror called al-Mir'at, scissors called al-Jami', and a staff of shawhat wood called al-Mamshuq."
"9409","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to strive in the path of Allah, for striving in the path of Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, is a gate among the gates of Paradise through which Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, delivers from worry and grief. > **""Strive in the path of Allah, for striving in the path of Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, is a gate among the gates of Paradise through which Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, delivers from worry and grief.""**"
"9410","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to hold to jihad in the path of Allah, for it is a gate among the gates of Paradise through which Allah removes worry and grief. > **""Hold to jihad in the path of Allah, for it is a gate among the gates of Paradise through which Allah removes worry and grief.""**"
"9411","Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, set out with the people before the campaign of Tabuk, led them in the dawn prayer when morning came, and then the people mounted. When the sun rose the people grew drowsy after the night journey, and Mu'adh kept close to the Messenger, following his tracks, while the people's mounts scattered from them to the sides of the road, grazing and walking. As Mu'adh followed the Messenger with his she-camel alternately grazing and walking, the she-camel of Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, stumbled, so Bilal jerked it with the rein and it sprang forward until it came near the she-camel of the Messenger. The Messenger then uncovered his face and looked around to find no one in the army nearer to him than Mu'adh, so he called out to him, O Mu'adh, and Mu'adh answered that he was at his service. The Messenger told him to draw nearer, so he drew near until their two mounts touched one another. The Messenger remarked that he had not imagined the people to be as far from them as they were, and Mu'adh explained that the people had grown drowsy and their mounts had scattered, grazing and walking, to which the Messenger replied that he too had grown drowsy. When Mu'adh saw the cheerfulness of the Messenger and his privacy with him, he asked permission to ask about a matter that had sickened, wearied and grieved him. The Messenger told him to ask whatever he wished, so Mu'adh asked him to tell him of a deed that would admit him into Paradise, saying he would ask about nothing else. The Messenger exclaimed thrice in approval and said he had asked about a momentous matter, yet one easy for whomever Allah intends good, repeating everything thrice out of eagerness that Mu'adh should master it. The Prophet then told him to believe in Allah and the Last Day, establish prayer, give zakat, and worship Allah alone without associating anything with Him until death upon that state, and repeated this thrice at Mu'adh's request. The Messenger then offered to tell him of the head of this affair, its pillar, and the peak of its hump, and upon Mu'adh's eager acceptance explained that its head is testifying that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah alone without partner and that Muhammad is His slave and Messenger, its pillar is establishing prayer and giving zakat, and the peak of its hump is jihad in the path of Allah. He added that he had been commanded to fight the people until they establish prayer, give zakat, and testify to the unity of Allah and the messengership of Muhammad, for when they do so they secure their blood and wealth except by its right, and their reckoning is upon Allah. The Messenger further swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that no face grows pale nor foot grows dusty in a deed seeking the degrees of Paradise after the obligatory prayer like jihad in the path of Allah, and that nothing weighs down the scale of a servant like a mount spent upon for him in the path of Allah or mounted in the path of Allah. > **""Ask whatever you wish. Excellent, excellent, excellent, you have asked about a momentous matter, yet it is easy for whomever Allah intends good. Believe in Allah and the Last Day, establish prayer, give zakat, and worship Allah alone without associating anything with Him until you die upon that. The head of this affair is testifying that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah alone without partner and that Muhammad is His slave and Messenger, its pillar is establishing prayer and giving zakat, and the peak of its hump is jihad in the path of Allah. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, no face grows pale nor foot grows dusty in a deed seeking the degrees of Paradise after the obligatory prayer like jihad in the path of Allah.""**"
"9412","Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that Islam is three dwellings: a lower, an upper, and a loft. The lower is Islam itself into which the generality of Muslims enter, so that none of them is asked but says that he is a Muslim. The upper is the superiority of their deeds, some Muslims being more excellent than others. The highest loft is jihad in the path of Allah, which none attain except the most excellent of them. > **""Islam is three dwellings: a lower, an upper, and a loft. As for the lower, it is Islam into which the generality of Muslims enter, so none of them is asked but says: I am a Muslim. As for the upper, it is the superiority of their deeds, some Muslims being more excellent than others. As for the highest loft, it is jihad in the path of Allah, which none attain except the most excellent of them.""**"
"9413","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that the peak of the hump of Islam is jihad, which none attain except the most excellent of them. > **""The peak of the hump of Islam is jihad, which none attain except the most excellent of them.""**"
"9414","Mu'adh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a woman came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said that her husband had gone out as a warrior while she used to follow him in his prayer when he prayed and in all his deeds, so she asked to be told of a deed that would bring her to his deed until he returned. The Prophet asked her whether she could stand without sitting, fast without breaking fast, and remember Allah without flagging until he returned. When she replied that she could not bear that, he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that even if she could bear it she would not reach a tenth of his deed. > **""Can you stand without sitting, fast without breaking fast, and remember Allah, the Exalted, without flagging until he returns? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, even if you could bear it you would not reach a tenth of his deed.""**"
"9415","Abdullah ibn Muhammad, Umar and Ammar the sons of Hafs reported from their fathers from their grandfathers that Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said, O successor of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I heard the Messenger say that the most excellent deed of the believers is jihad in the path of Allah, and that he wished to bind himself in the path of Allah until death. Abu Bakr adjured him by Allah and by his sanctity and right, saying that he had grown old, his strength had weakened, and his end had drawn near, so Bilal remained with him. When Abu Bakr died, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said the like to Bilal, but Bilal refused him, so Umar asked him to whom the adhan should then go. Bilal named Sa'd, for he had called the adhan at Quba in the time of the Messenger, so Umar entrusted the adhan to Uqbah and Sa'd. > **""The most excellent deed of the believers is jihad in the path of Allah.""**"
"9416","Jidar, a man from among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, reported that they went out on campaign with the Messenger, and when they met their enemy he stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, then addressed the people that they stood between green, yellow and red spoils and provisions in their saddle-bags, and that when they met their enemy they should advance relentlessly, for none charges in the path of Allah but two of the wide-eyed maidens of Paradise hasten to him, and when he is martyred the first drop of his blood to fall to the earth brings him Allah's expiation of every sin, while the two maidens wipe the dust from his face saying that his time has come, and he answers that their time too has come. > **""O people, when you meet your enemy, advance, advance, for none charges in the path of Allah but two of the wide-eyed maidens of Paradise hasten to him. Your time has come.""**"
"9417","Abu Malik al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever establishes prayer, gives zakat, and dies worshipping Allah without associating anything with Him, it is binding upon Allah to admit him into Paradise, whether he emigrated or remained in his birthplace. When a man asked whether he should convey this to the people so they might rest upon it, the Messenger explained that Allah has prepared one hundred degrees for the mujahidin in His path, between every two degrees the distance between heaven and earth, and that had he the means to spend and strengthen the Muslims, or had they the means to spend, no expedition would go forth but he would accompany it, yet neither he nor they had the means. He added that were he to go forth, none capable would remain behind but would go with him, which would be hard upon him and upon them, and that he wished to fight and be killed, then be brought to life, then fight and be killed, then be brought to life and be killed. > **""Whoever establishes prayer, gives zakat, and dies worshipping Allah without associating anything with Him, it is binding upon Allah to admit him into Paradise, whether he emigrated or remained in his birthplace. Indeed Allah has prepared one hundred degrees for the mujahidin in His path, between every two degrees as between heaven and earth. I wish to fight and be killed, then be brought to life, then fight and be killed, then be brought to life and be killed.""**"
"9418","Al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that the example of the mujahid in the path of Allah is that of the one fasting by day and standing in prayer by night until the warrior returns, whenever he returns. > **""The example of the mujahid in the path of Allah is like that of the fasting person by day and the standing person by night until he returns, whenever he returns.""**"
"9419","Abu Hind, a man from among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that the example of the mujahid in the path of Allah is that of the fasting and devout worshipper who does not flag in fasting, prayer or charity. > **""The example of the mujahid in the path of Allah is like that of the fasting and devout worshipper who does not flag in fasting, prayer or charity.""**"
"9420","Amr ibn Abasah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that whoever fights in the path of Allah even for the time between two milkings of a she-camel, Allah forbids the Fire for his face. > **""Whoever fights in the path of Allah even for the time of milking a she-camel, Allah forbids the Fire for his face.""**"
"9421","A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that a mukatab slave of hers came to her with the remainder of his contract sum, whereupon she told him that he would not enter upon her after this occasion and urged him to jihad in the path of Allah, for she had heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that no dust in the path of Allah mixes with the heart of a Muslim man but Allah forbids him to the Fire. > **""No dust in the path of Allah mixes with the heart of a Muslim man but Allah forbids him to the Fire.""**"
"9422","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said that when the heart of the believer trembles in the path of Allah, his sins fall away from him as the date-clusters of the palm fall. > **""When the heart of the believer trembles in the path of Allah, his sins fall away from him as the date-clusters of the palm fall.""**"
"9423","Abu al-Mundhir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said that a certain man had died and asked him to pray over him, whereupon Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, objected that the man was dissolute and should not be prayed over. The man then asked the Messenger whether he had not seen the night in which he had entered the morning while on guard, for the deceased had been among the guards. The Messenger then stood, prayed over him, followed him to his grave, sat until the burial was finished, threw three handfuls of earth upon him, and said that people praised evil of him while he praised good of him. When Umar asked the reason, the Prophet told him to leave that aside, O son of al-Khattab, for whoever strives in the path of Allah has Paradise made obligatory for him. > **""People praise evil of you and I praise good of you. Leave us aside, O son of al-Khattab, whoever strives in the path of Allah, Paradise has become obligatory for him.""**"
"9424","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say that when the warrior goes out in the path of Allah his sins are made a bridge upon the door of his house, so that when he leaves it behind he leaves all his sins behind with not even a gnat's wing of them remaining upon him. Allah guarantees him four things: to guard what he leaves behind of family and wealth, to admit him into Paradise by whatever death he dies, to return him safely with whatever reward or spoils he attains by whatever return he returns, and that no sun sets but it sets with his sins. > **""When the warrior goes out in the path of Allah, his sins are made a bridge upon the door of his house, so when he leaves it behind he leaves all his sins behind with not even a gnat's wing of them remaining upon him. Allah guarantees him four things: to guard what he leaves behind of family and wealth, to admit him into Paradise by whatever death he dies, to return him safely with whatever reward or spoils he attains, and that no sun sets but it sets with his sins.""**"
"9425","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ ordered a detachment to set out, and the men asked whether to leave that night or wait till morning. > **""Do you not love to spend the night in the harvest of Paradise?""**"
"9426","Abdullah ibn Atik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger ﷺ speak about one who leaves his house as a warrior in the path of Allah Almighty. The Prophet ﷺ joined his three fingers, the middle, the index, and the thumb, and asked where the warriors are. > **""If he falls from his mount and dies, his reward is binding upon Allah. If a creature stings him and he dies, his reward is binding upon Allah. If he dies a natural death, his reward is binding upon Allah Almighty. By Allah, it is a phrase I never heard from any Arab before the Messenger ﷺ. If he dies, his reward is binding upon Allah, and if he is killed and fulfills his duty, he has earned the best return.""**"
"9427","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ entrusted them with five deeds, any one of which brings the guarantee of Allah Almighty. > **""Whoever visits a sick person, or accompanies a funeral, or goes out as a warrior in the path of Allah, or enters upon a ruler intending to support and honor him, or sits in his house remaining safe while people remain safe from him.""**"
"9428","Humayd ibn Hilal reported from a man of Tufawah who used to pass by their tribe and narrate. The man came to Madinah with a caravan, sold his goods, then came to the Messenger ﷺ, who showed him a house and described a woman who had gone out with a Muslim detachment, leaving twelve goats and her weaving tool behind. > **""She missed one of her goats and her tool, so she said, My Lord, You have guaranteed to guard one who goes out in Your path, yet I have lost a goat and my tool, so I adjure You by my goat and my tool. In the morning she found her goat and another like it with it, and her tool and another like it with it. Go to her and ask her if you wish.""**"
"9429","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported from the Prophet ﷺ about six traits, any one of which faithfully kept makes Allah guarantee Paradise for a Muslim. > **""A man who goes out as a warrior and dies on his way has guarantee from Allah. A man who follows a funeral and dies on his way has guarantee from Allah. A man who performs ablution well then goes to a mosque for prayer and dies on his way has guarantee from Allah. And a man in his house who does not backbite Muslims nor bring anger or vengeance upon them, and dies in that state, has guarantee from Allah.""**"
"9430","Muhammad ibn Hatib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ addressed one deprived of family comfort. > **""When one of you is deprived of wife and child, then jihad is binding upon him.""**"
"9431","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described the spiritual devotion of nations, with another wording mentioning every Prophet. > **""Every nation has a monasticism, and the monasticism of this nation is jihad in the path of Allah.""**"
"9432","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described the travel and devotion of his nation. > **""Every nation has a journey, and the journey of my nation is jihad in the path of Allah. Every nation has a monasticism, and the monasticism of my nation is guarding in the face of the enemy.""**"
"9433","Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, said this on the day of Siffin to encourage steadfastness, comparing the thirsty standing firm until water reaches its watering places to the nearness of Paradise beneath the struggle."
"9434","Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while traveling with the Messenger ﷺ they heard people asking which deeds are best, and then they heard a caller in the valley pronouncing the testimony of faith. > **""Faith in Allah and His Messenger, jihad in the path of Allah, and an accepted Hajj. And I too bear witness, and I bear witness that no one testifies to it except that he is freed from polytheism.""**"
"9435","Al-Shifa bint Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with her, who was among the migrant women, reported that the Messenger ﷺ was asked about the best deeds. > **""Faith in Allah, jihad in the path of Allah, and an accepted Hajj.""**"
"9436","Ubadah ibn Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while he was with the Messenger ﷺ a man came asking which deeds are best, and as the man turned to leave the Prophet ﷺ added easier counsels twice. > **""Faith in Allah, jihad in His path, and an accepted Hajj. Easier for you than that is to feed food, speak gently, and show good character. Easier for you than that is not to accuse Allah concerning anything He has decreed against you.""**"
"9437","In another wording of the previous report, the inquiring man himself asked for something still easier, and the Messenger ﷺ answered briefly. > **""Gentleness and patience.""**"
"9438","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ thrice asked whether to inform them, and the people replied affirmatively each time. > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of mankind? A man who holds his horse's bridle in the path of Allah, mounting whenever he hears an alarm. Shall I inform you of the one next to him? A man among a flock of sheep who establishes prayer and pays charity. Shall I not inform you of the worst of mankind? One who is asked by Allah and does not give for His sake.""**"
"9439","Aishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked which deeds are best. > **""Faith in Allah, jihad in the path of Allah, and an accepted Hajj.""**"
"9440","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported from the Prophet ﷺ about the mutual superiority of Hajj and military expeditions, explaining the case after the obligatory Hajj. > **""One Hajj is better than forty expeditions, and one expedition is better than forty Hajjs. When a man has performed the Hajj of Islam, then an expedition is better for him than forty Hajjs, and the Hajj of Islam is better than forty expeditions.""**"
"9441","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they set out with the Messenger ﷺ on a detachment when a man passed a cave containing water and nearby greens and considered staying there apart from the world, then decided to consult the Prophet ﷺ and asked permission. > **""I was not sent with Judaism nor with Christianity, but I was sent with the easy upright way. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, a morning march or an evening march in the path of Allah is better than the world and all it contains, and the standing of one of you in the rank is better than his prayer for sixty years.""**"
"9442","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a Companion passed a mountain pass with water, admired its pleasantness, and thought of withdrawing there, but said he would not do so until consulting the Messenger ﷺ. > **""Do not do so, for the standing of one of you in the path of Allah is better for him than his standing in his house for sixty years. Whoever fights in the path of Allah for the time between two milkings of a camel, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"9443","Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah reported from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man asked whether he had heard anything from the Messenger ﷺ about horses, and he affirmed and quoted the counsel about buying and borrowing upon Allah and about jihad remaining fresh. > **""Good is tied in the forelocks of horses till the Day of Resurrection. Buy upon Allah and borrow upon Allah. Say, we borrow against our share and we buy till Allah grants us victory. You will remain in good while your striving stays fresh. In the last age there will be people who doubt striving, so strive in their time, then raid, for raiding then is freshest.""**"
"9444","Al-Falatan ibn Asim, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when revelation began to descend, recognized by his fixed gaze, open eyes, and full attention of hearing and heart. The Prophet ﷺ told the scribe to write that the sitters and the warriors are not equal, whereupon a blind man stood asking about his fault, and further revelation came while he stood seeking refuge. > **""Write: Those who sit among the believers and those who strive in the path of Allah are not equal. Write: Except those with injury.""**"
"9445","Abu Musab reported that an elderly Medinan arrived with visibly prepared gear, and when asked he said he intended the west, having heard the Messenger ﷺ speak of people heading west. > **""People will go out toward the west and will come on the Day of Resurrection with their faces upon the light of the sun.""**"
"9446","Amr ibn al-Hamiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ foretold a tribulation and identified the safest people in it, which is why Ibn al-Hamiq said he had come to the people of Egypt. > **""There will be a tribulation in which the safest of people will be the western army.""**"
"9447","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger ﷺ once rested his head in the house of one of his wives, slept, smiled in his sleep, and when awakened and asked by his wife what made him smile, he spoke of people of his nation upon the sea. > **""I wondered at people of my nation who ride this sea around the enemy, striving in the path of Allah Almighty.""**"
"9448","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger ﷺ compared Hajj, land expeditions, and sea expeditions, and described the sea traveler and the seasick. > **""A Hajj for one who has not performed Hajj is better than ten expeditions, and an expedition for one who has performed Hajj is better than ten Hajjs. An expedition at sea is better than ten expeditions on land. Whoever crosses the sea, it is as if he crossed all the valleys. The seasick is like one rolling in his blood.""**"
"9449","Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described one sea raid in the path of Allah, while Allah best knows who truly strives in His path. > **""Whoever raids once at sea in the path of Allah has rendered to Allah all his obedience, sought Paradise by every seeking, and fled the Fire by every fleeing.""**"
"9450","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ gave direction to one who missed fighting alongside him. > **""Whoever missed fighting alongside me, let him fight at sea.""**"
"9451","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised this report to the Prophet ﷺ about Allah addressing the two seas and asking what they would do with His servants carried upon them. > **""Allah spoke to this western sea and spoke to the eastern sea. He said to the western sea, I shall carry in you servants of Mine, so what will you do with them? It said, I shall drown them. He said, your harm is in your regions, so He deprived it of ornament and catch. Then He spoke to this eastern sea and said, I shall carry in you servants of Mine, so what will you do with them? It said, I shall carry them upon my body and be to them like a mother to her child, so He rewarded it with ornament and catch.""**"
"9452","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ restricted sea travel. > **""Do not ride the sea except as a pilgrim or as a warrior.""**"
"9453","Thawban, freedman of the Messenger ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ gave glad tidings of two protected groups. > **""Two bands of my nation Allah will shield from the Fire: a band that invades India, and a band that will be with Isa son of Maryam.""**"
"9454","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ gave glad tidings to those who remember Allah much during striving, and when asked about spending he said it follows the same measure. Abdurrahman then asked Muadh whether spending is only seven hundredfold, and Muadh corrected his understanding. > **""Glad tidings for whoever remembers Allah much in jihad in the path of Allah, for he has for every word seventy thousand good deeds, each good deed multiplied tenfold besides what he has with Allah of increase. Spending follows that same measure. That seven hundredfold is when they spend while staying among their families without raiding. When they raid and spend, Allah stores for them from the treasures of His mercy what the knowledge and description of servants cannot reach. Those are the party of Allah, and the party of Allah are the victorious.""**"
"9455","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, stated that spending in the path of Allah is multiplied seven hundredfold."
"9456","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described three who die after setting out. > **""Whoever goes out for Hajj and dies, the reward of a pilgrim is written for him till the Day of Resurrection. Whoever goes out for Umrah and dies, he has the reward of one performing Umrah. Whoever goes out as a warrior and dies, the reward of a warrior is written for him till the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9457","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger ﷺ speak of one thrown in Allah's path. > **""Whoever is thrown from his mount in the path of Allah and dies, he is a martyr.""**"
"9458","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ spoke of one who equips or represents a warrior among his family. > **""Whoever equips a warrior or represents him among his family with good, he is with us.""**"
"9459","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ equated equipping and caretaking with fighting. > **""Whoever equips a warrior in the path of Allah has fought, and whoever represents him among his family with good has fought.""**"
"9460","Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ about the reward of equipping and spending on the family. > **""Whoever equips a warrior in the path of Allah has the like of his reward. Whoever represents a warrior among his family with good and spends upon his family has the like of his reward.""**"
"9461","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that in the year of Bani Lihyan the Messenger ﷺ ordered shared participation. > **""Let one man from every two of you go out, and let the warrior be represented among his family and wealth, and he shall have the like of half his reward.""**"
"9462","Jabalah ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Prophet ﷺ himself did not go to battle, he entrusted his arms. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ did not raid, he would give his weapon to Ali or to Usamah.""**"
"9463","Sahl ibn Hunayf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ promised the shade of Allah to one who helps three kinds of people. > **""Whoever helps a warrior in the path of Allah, or a debtor in his hardship, or a contracted slave concerning his freedom, Allah will shade him on a Day when there is no shade but His.""**"
"9464","Amr ibn Mirdas reported that he came to Syria and found a man with thick lips and nose, with weapons before him, urging people to take weapons, repair them, and strive, quoting the Messenger ﷺ. When asked who he was, they said it was Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him. > **""O people, take from these weapons, repair them, and strive with them in the path of Allah.""**"
"9465","Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ promised shade and like reward for two services to a warrior. > **""Whoever shades the head of a warrior, Allah will shade him on the Day of Resurrection, a Day when there is no shade but His. Whoever equips a warrior until he is fully equipped shall have the like of his reward.""**"
"9466","Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said he would rather grant even a whip for use in the path of Allah than perform one Hajj after another."
"9467","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ warned households wholly absent from striving and support. > **""There is no household from whom no warrior goes out, nor one who equips a warrior even with a thread or a needle or its worth in silver, nor one who represents him among his family with good, except that Allah will strike them with calamity before the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9468","Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ warned against collective abandonment of striving. > **""No people abandon jihad except that Allah covers them with punishment.""**"
"9469","Muadh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger ﷺ that he ordered his Companions to go out, whereupon a man told his family he would stay behind to pray noon with the Messenger ﷺ, greet him, bid farewell, and seek a prayer that would intercede on Judgment Day. After the prayer the man came greeting, and the Prophet ﷺ asked him whether he knew how far his companions had preceded him. > **""Do you know by how much your companions have preceded you? By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, they have preceded you in virtue by farther than what is between the two easts and the two wests.""**"
"9470","Muawiyah ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger ﷺ praise a short march. > **""A morning march in the path of Allah or an evening march is better than the world and all it contains.""**"
"9471","Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was beneath the shade of the mount of the Messenger ﷺ on the day of the Farewell Hajj, or that a man narrated it to him, while the Messenger ﷺ was upon the saddle. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had conveyed, they thought he meant them and replied affirmatively, and he repeated it three times, adding counsel about marching and sanctity. > **""Have I conveyed? An evening march in the path of Allah is better than the world and all upon it. A morning march in the path of Allah is better than the world and all upon it. The honor, wealth, and soul of a believer are forbidden to another believer, sacred like the sanctity of this day.""**"
"9472","Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ praised a short march in Allah's path. > **""A morning march or an evening march in the path of Allah is better than the world and all it contains.""**"
"9473","Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ praised a short march in Allah's path. > **""A morning march in the path of Allah or an evening march is better than the world and all it contains.""**"
"9474","Abu Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, raised this report to the Prophet ﷺ about dust, dusty feet, fasting, wounds, and a brief fight. > **""Allah Almighty does not join in the belly of a man dust in the path of Allah and the smoke of Hell. Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids the rest of his body to the Fire. Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah distances the Fire from him a thousand years march for a hastening rider. Whoever is wounded in the path of Allah is sealed with the seal of martyrs, a light on Judgment Day whose color is like saffron and whose scent is like musk, by which the first and the last recognize him and say, this one bears the stamp of martyrs. Whoever fights in the path of Allah Almighty for the time of milking a camel, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"9475","Abu al-Musabbih reported that while they were marching at Darb Qalmat, the commander Malik ibn Abdullah al-Khathami called to a man leading his horse along the mountain side, asking why he would not ride, and the man cited what he had heard from the Messenger ﷺ. > **""Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, they are forbidden to the Fire.""**"
"9476","Malik ibn Abdullah al-Khathami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ spoke of dusty feet. > **""Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids him to the Fire.""**"
"9477","Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ spoke of dusty feet, after which the narrator said he never saw more walking men than that day while they were behind the passes. > **""Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids him to the Fire.""**"
"9478","Sulayman ibn Musa reported that Malik ibn Abdullah al-Khathami, while commanding people on a summer campaign in Roman land, was leading his mount when asked to ride since his mount appeared strong-backed, and he cited the Messenger ﷺ. Thereupon Malik and the people dismounted and walked, and no day with more walkers was seen. > **""No feet of a servant become dusty in the path of Allah except that Allah forbids the Fire upon them.""**"
"9479","Abdullah ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Zaynab reported that Malik ibn Abdullah al-Juhani passed by Habib ibn Maslamah, or Habib passed by Malik, while he was leading his horse on foot. Asked why he would not ride though Allah had provided him a mount, he cited the Messenger ﷺ. > **""Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids him to the Fire.""**"
"9480","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ spoke of dusty feet. > **""Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids them to the Fire.""**"
"9481","Umar ibn Qays al-Kindi reported that they were with Abu Darda returning from a summer campaign when he called people together and cited the Messenger ﷺ. > **""Whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah forbids the rest of his body to the Fire.""**"
"9482","Ubadah ibn Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger ﷺ speak of path dust and Hell smoke. > **""Dust in the path of Allah and the smoke of Hell never join in the belly of a Muslim man.""**"
"9483","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ spoke of path dust and Hell smoke. > **""Dust in the path of Allah and the smoke of Hell never join in the nostrils of a servant.""**"
"9484","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ about dusty face and dusty feet. > **""No man whose face becomes dusty in the path of Allah except that Allah secures him from the smoke of the Fire on Judgment Day. No man whose feet become dusty in the path of Allah except that Allah secures his feet from the Fire on Judgment Day.""**"
"9485","Rabi ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while the Messenger ﷺ was walking along the road he saw a Quraysh youth walking apart. The Prophet ﷺ asked them to call him, asked why he had kept aside, and when the youth said he disliked dust, he counseled him. > **""Do not keep away from it, for by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it is the fragrance of Paradise.""**"
"9486","Abu Rayhanah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that they were with the Messenger ﷺ on an expedition and spent a night upon a hill in severe cold, so that men dug hollows and covered themselves with shields. Seeing this, the Messenger ﷺ called for one to guard them that night in return for a virtuous supplication. An Ansari volunteered, was called near, named himself, and received abundant supplication. Abu Rayhanah then volunteered second and received a lesser supplication, followed by counsel about eyes forbidden to the Fire. > **""Who will guard us tonight, that I may supplicate for him with a supplication containing virtue? The Fire is forbidden for an eye that shed tears from fear of Allah. The Fire is forbidden for an eye that stayed awake in the path of Allah.""**"
"9487","Muadh ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger ﷺ about one who guards behind the Muslims voluntarily for Allah, uncompelled by any ruler. > **""Whoever guards behind the Muslims in the path of Allah Almighty voluntarily, uncompelled by a ruler, his two eyes will not see the Fire except to fulfill the oath, for Allah Almighty says, There is none of you but will approach it.""**"
"9488","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described two eyes. > **""Two eyes the Fire will never touch: an eye that spent the night guarding the Muslims in the path of Allah, and an eye that wept from fear of Allah.""**"
"9489","Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described two eyes. > **""Two eyes the Fire will not touch: an eye that wept in the depth of night from fear of Allah Almighty, and an eye that spent the night guarding in the path of Allah Almighty.""**"
"9490","Muawiyah ibn Haydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described three eyes. > **""Three whose eyes will not see the Fire: an eye that guarded in the path of Allah, an eye that wept from fear of Allah, and an eye that refrained from what Allah has forbidden.""**"
"9491","Abu Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ spoke of sitting by the sea with sincere intention to protect Muslims. > **""Whoever sits by the sea seeking reward, with good intention, as a precaution for the Muslims, Allah writes for him a good deed for every drop in the sea.""**"
"9492","Abu Atiyyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ sat and mentioned that a man had died, asking whether anyone had seen him upon any good deed. A man said he had guarded one night with him in Allah's path, so the Messenger ﷺ and those with him stood, prayed over him, and when he was placed in the grave the Prophet ﷺ threw soil with his hand and addressed him, then counseled Umar ibn al-Khattab. > **""Your companions think you are among the people of the Fire, and I bear witness that you are among the people of Paradise. Do not ask about the deeds of people, but ask about their natural disposition.""**"
"9493","Qurrah ibn Iyas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ described one loud takbir at the seashore at sunset. > **""Whoever pronounces one takbir upon the seashore at sunset, raising his voice, Allah gives him in reward ten good deeds for every drop in the sea, erases ten sins from him, and raises him ten ranks, with a hundred years march by a swift horse between each two ranks.""**"
"9494","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger ﷺ compared guarding to fasting and standing. > **""Guarding for a day is better than fasting a month and standing it in prayer.""**"
"9495","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ said the deed of every dead person is sealed except the guard, with another wording about security from the tempter of the grave. > **""Every dead person has his deed sealed except the one guarding in the path of Allah, for his reward flows for him till Allah raises him. And he is secured from the tempter of the grave.""**"
"9496","Umm Darda, may Allah be pleased with her, raised this report to the Prophet ﷺ about guarding at Muslim coasts. > **""Whoever guards for three days at any of the coasts of the Muslims, it suffices him for a year of guarding.""**"
"9497","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger ﷺ about guarding one night at the seashore, explaining the year as three hundred sixty days and each day as a thousand years. > **""Whoever guards one night upon the seashore is better than his worship among his family for a thousand years.""**"
"9498","Uthman ibn Affan and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported from the Prophet ﷺ about one who dies while guarding. > **""Whoever dies while guarding in the path of Allah, the deed of a fasting person flows for him, his provision flows for him, he is safe from the tempter, and Allah will raise him on Judgment Day safe from the greatest terror.""**"
"9499","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger ﷺ was asked about the reward of guarding and answered about one day of protection behind the Muslims. > **""Whoever guards for a day as protection behind the Muslims shall have the reward of those behind him who fasted and prayed.""**"
"9500","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""Whoever guards the frontier for a day in the path of Allah, Allah places between him and the Fire seven trenches, each trench like seven heavens and seven earths.""**"
"9501","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Whoever guards the frontier in the path of Allah, Allah secures him from the trial of the grave.""**"
"9502","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Whoever fasts a day in the path of Allah, Allah distances him from the Fire by seventy autumns. Whoever dies while guarding the frontier is shielded from the trial of the grave, and his provision continues to flow for him.""**"
"9503","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Complete guarding of the frontier is forty days. Whoever guards for forty days, neither buying nor selling nor committing any wrongdoing, leaves his sins behind like the day his mother bore him.""**"
"9504","Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Guarding the frontier for a month is better than fasting a lifetime. Whoever dies while guarding in the path of Allah is safe from the Greatest Terror; his provision is brought to him morning and evening, he breathes the scent of Paradise, and the reward of the warrior runs for him until Allah raises him.""**"
"9505","Al-Irbad ibn Sariyah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Every deed is cut off from its owner when he dies, except the one who guards the frontier in the path of Allah. His deed keeps growing for him, and his provision keeps flowing for him until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9506","Shurahbil ibn al-Simt reported that he saw Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, guarding the frontier on the coast of Hims. He asked him what he was doing, and Salman replied that he was guarding. Salman then said he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""Guarding for a day in the path of Allah is like fasting a month and standing its nights in prayer. Whoever dies while guarding, the deed he used to do keeps flowing for him, he is safe from the tempter of the grave, and he is raised on the Day of Resurrection as a martyr.""**"
"9507","Utbah ibn al-Nuddar, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""When your campaigns come to an end, spoils are treated as lawful booty to be misused, and fines and penalties multiply, then the best of your struggle is guarding the frontier.""**"
"9508","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The most excellent of those who go forth in the path of Allah is their servant, then the one who brings them news. The most distinguished of them in rank with Allah is the fasting one. Whoever draws a waterskin for his companions in the path of Allah outstrips them toward Paradise by seventy degrees.""**"
"9509","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, conveyed from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: > **""The most excellent struggle is that of the one whose horse is hamstrung and whose blood is shed.""**"
"9510","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: it was asked, O Messenger of Allah, which Islam is most excellent? He said: > **""The one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe.""** It was asked, which migration is most excellent? He said: > **""That you abandon what your Lord dislikes.""** It was asked, which struggle is most excellent? He said: > **""That of the one whose horse is hamstrung and whose blood is shed.""**"
"9511","Utbah ibn Abd al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, one of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The slain are three. A believing man who strives with his soul and his wealth in the path of Allah until he meets the enemy and fights them till he is killed: that is the boastful martyr in the tent of Allah beneath His Throne; the Prophets surpass him only by the rank of prophethood. A believing man who wronged himself with sins and faults, then strives with his soul and wealth in the path of Allah until he meets the enemy and fights till he is killed: anguish wipes away his sins and faults, for the sword erases faults, and he enters through whichever of the gates of Paradise he wishes, for it has eight gates and Hell has seven, some better than others. And a hypocrite who strives with his soul and wealth until he meets the enemy and fights in the path of Allah till he is killed: that one is in the Fire, for the sword does not wipe away hypocrisy.""**"
"9512","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyrs are three. A man who goes out with his soul and wealth seeking reward in the path of Allah, not intending to fight or be killed but to swell the numbers of the Muslims: if he dies or is killed, all his sins are forgiven, he is shielded from the punishment of the grave, secured from terror, married to maidens of Paradise, clothed in the robe of honor, and a crown of dignity and eternity is placed on his head. The second goes out with his soul and wealth seeking reward, intending to be killed but not to kill: if he dies or is killed, his knee is beside Ibrahim, the close friend of the Merciful, before Allah in a seat of truth near the All-Powerful King. The third goes out with his soul and wealth seeking reward, intending to kill and be killed: if he dies or is killed, he comes on the Day of Resurrection brandishing his sword upon his shoulder while the people kneel, saying, Make way for us, for we spent our blood for Allah. By Him in whose hand is my soul, were he to say that to Ibrahim or any Prophet, they would yield the path to him for the due right they see is his, until they reach pulpits of light beneath the Throne and sit upon them, watching how judgment is passed among the people. They feel no pangs of death, they do not tarry in the barrier between worlds, the Blast does not frighten them, the Reckoning, the Balance, and the Bridge do not trouble them. They only watch how judgment is passed. They ask for nothing but it is given them, they intercede for nothing but their intercession is accepted, and they are given of Paradise whatever they love and settle wherever they love of it.""**"
"9513","Nuaym ibn Hammar, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: a man asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, which martyrs are most excellent? He said: > **""Those who, when drawn up in the rank, do not turn their faces away until they are killed. They hasten about in the highest chambers of Paradise, and their Lord laughs toward them. When your Lord laughs toward a servant in this world, there is no reckoning upon him.""**"
"9514","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The most excellent struggle before Allah on the Day of Resurrection is that of those who meet the enemy in the first rank and do not turn their faces away until they are killed. They roll about in the highest chambers of Paradise. Their Lord gazes at them, and when He laughs toward a people, there is no reckoning upon them.""**"
"9515","Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Being killed in the path of Allah expiates all sins, or all things, except the trust. There is a trust in prayer, a trust in fasting, a trust in speech, and the severest of it concerns deposits held in keeping.""**"
"9516","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyr has with Allah six qualities: he is forgiven with the first shedding of his blood, he sees his seat in Paradise, he is robed in the garment of faith, he is married to maidens of Paradise, he is shielded from the punishment of the grave, he is safe from the Greatest Terror, a crown of dignity is placed on his head whose single ruby is better than the world and all in it, he is married to seventy-two wives of the maidens of Paradise, and he intercedes for seventy of his kin.""**"
"9517","Qays al-Judhami, may Allah be pleased with him, a man who had the honor of Companionship, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyr is given six qualities at the first drop of his blood: every fault is expiated from him, he sees his seat in Paradise, he is married to maidens of Paradise, he is secured from the Greatest Terror and the punishment of the grave, and he is robed in the garment of faith.""**"
"9518","Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyr has six qualities: he is forgiven with the first shedding of his blood, he is secured from terror, he sees his seat in Paradise, he is married to maidens of Paradise, and he is shielded from the punishment of the grave.""**"
"9519","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""With the first drop that falls of the martyr's blood, his sins are expiated. With the second, he is clothed in garments of faith. With the third, he is married to maidens of Paradise.""**"
"9520","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyr is forgiven with the first gush of his blood, married to two maidens, and intercedes for seventy of his household. The one who guards the frontier, when he dies at his post, has the reward of his deed written for him until the Day of Resurrection; provision is brought to him morning and evening, he is married to seventy maidens, and it is said to him: Stand and intercede until the Reckoning is finished.""**"
"9521","Yazid ibn Shajarah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""You have come to dwell among red and green and yellow. When you meet your enemy, then forward, forward. No one charges in the path of Allah but two maidens of Paradise rush toward him. When he is martyred, the first drop of his blood falls and Allah wipes away every sin from him. The two wipe the dust from his face, saying: Your time has come. And he says: And yours has come.""**"
"9522","Mujahid reported from Yazid ibn Shajarah, may Allah be pleased with him, a man whose deed confirmed his word, that he addressed the people: O people, remember Allah's favor upon you. Then he said: when the people are arrayed for prayer and arrayed for battle, the doors of heaven are opened, the doors of Paradise are opened, the doors of Fire are opened, and the maidens of Paradise are adorned and look out. When a man advances they say, O Allah, aid him. When he turns away they veil themselves from him and say, O Allah, forgive him. So strike the faces of the enemy; my father and mother be ransom for you. Do not disgrace the maidens of Paradise. The first drop that spills wipes away all he ever did. Two wives from among the maidens descend to him, wipe his face, and say, Your time has come, and he says, And yours has come. Then he is clothed in a hundred robes, not woven by the children of Adam but grown in Paradise; were they placed between two fingers they would fit therein. And he used to say: I was told that swords are the keys of Paradise."
"9523","Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyr feels the pain of being killed only as one of you feels the touch of an ant's bite.""**"
"9524","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyrs dwell upon Bariq, a river at the gate of Paradise in a green dome. Their provision comes out to them from Paradise morning and evening.""**"
"9525","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: a man came to the prayer while the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was praying. When he reached the rank he said, O Allah, grant me what You grant Your righteous servants. When the Messenger of Allah finished, he asked: > **""Who was speaking just now?""** The man said, I was, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""Then your horse shall be hamstrung and you shall be martyred.""**"
"9526","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say to us: > **""Whoever of you is killed, patient and advancing, killed in the path of Allah, he is in Paradise.""**"
"9527","Kab ibn Ujrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: one day the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to his Companions: > **""What do you say of a man killed in the path of Allah?""** They said, Paradise. The Messenger of Allah said: > **""Paradise, if Allah wills.""** He said: > **""What do you say of a man who dies, and two just men stand and say: We know only good of him?""** They said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: > **""Paradise, if Allah wills.""** He said: > **""What do you say of a man who dies, and two just men stand and say: We know no good of him?""** They said, the Fire. The Messenger of Allah said: > **""A sinner, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.""**"
"9528","Abu Qatadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyr feels the pain of being killed only as one of you feels the touch of an ant's bite.""**"
"9529","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, conveyed from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: > **""Whoever is killed seeking the Face of Allah, Allah will not punish him.""**"
"9530","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""When the servant stands for the Reckoning, a people will come with their swords laid upon their necks, dripping blood, crowding at the gate of Paradise. It will be asked: Who are these? It will be said: The martyrs; they were alive, provided for.""**"
"9531","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was on a campaign when a polytheist champion challenged a Muslim to single combat and killed him, then another Muslim stepped forward and the polytheist killed him too. Then the man came and stood before the Prophet and asked, For what do you fight? He said: > **""Our religion is that we fight the people until they testify that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, and that we fulfill Allah's due right.""** The man said, By Allah, this is good. I believe in this. Then he turned to the Muslims, charged the polytheists, and fought till he was killed. He was carried and laid beside the two companions he had killed before that. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""These are the most loving toward one another among the people of Paradise.""**"
"9532","Salma bint Jabir reported: her husband was martyred, so she came to Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: I am a woman whose husband was martyred, men have proposed to me, and I refused to marry until I meet him. Do you hope for me that when we are joined, I shall be among his wives? He said, Yes. A man beside him said, We have never seen you do this since we have sat with you. Ibn Masud said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""The swiftest of my nation to join me in Paradise is a woman of Ahmas.""**"
"9533","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The one slain in the path of Allah, advancing and not retreating, outstrips the slain one who retreated toward Paradise by seventy autumns, and outstrips the sick of my nation among their companions by seventy autumns. The Prophets enter before Sulayman son of Dawud by forty autumns, because of the kingship he held.""**"
"9534","Sad ibn Junadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyrs of the land are more excellent before Allah than the martyrs of the sea.""**"
"9535","Ibn Abi Umayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""No soul among the people whose Lord takes it, were it Muslim, wishes to return to you even if it had the world and all in it, except the martyr.""** And Ibn Abi Umayrah reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""To be killed in the path of Allah is dearer to me than that the settled folk and the nomads all belong to me.""**"
"9536","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prepared a campaign, so I came to him and said, O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah for martyrdom for me. The Messenger of Allah said: > **""O Allah, grant them spoils and keep them safe.""** So we returned safe with spoils. Then the Messenger prepared a second campaign, and I asked again for martyrdom, and he said: > **""O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils.""** Then he prepared a third campaign, and I said, O Messenger of Allah, I came to you twice before this asking you to supplicate for martyrdom, and you said, O Allah, keep them safe and grant them spoils, so I returned safe with spoils. O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah for martyrdom for me. So we returned safe with spoils. The rest of the account was mentioned in full in the Book of Fasting."
"9537","Abu Malik al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Whoever sincerely asks Allah from his soul to be killed in His path, then dies or is killed, has the reward of a martyr. Whoever is wounded in the path of Allah or suffers a bruise, it comes on the Day of Resurrection at its fullest: its color like saffron, its scent the scent of musk. Whoever bears wounds in the path of Allah carries upon him the seal of the martyrs.""**"
"9538","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported, in a narration deemed raised to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: > **""Whoever goes out in the path of Allah comes on the Day of Resurrection with his blood at its fullest, its color saffron, its scent the scent of musk, and upon him the seal of the martyrs.""**"
"9539","Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported: on the day of Uhud, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to his brother, Take my coat of mail, my brother. He replied, I desire the same martyrdom that you desire. So both of them left it behind."
"9540","Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported: when a servant is killed in the path of Allah, the first drop of his blood falls upon the earth and Allah wipes away all his sins. Then a cloth from Paradise is sent to him in which his soul is taken, and a body from Paradise until his spirit is joined to it. Then he ascends with the angels as though he had been with them since Allah created him, until he is brought to the heaven. He passes no gate but it is opened for him, and no angel but prays for mercy and forgiveness for him, until he is brought to the Most Merciful, so he prostrates before the angels and then the angels prostrate after him. Then he is forgiven and purified, then ordered toward the martyrs, and he finds them in green meadows and domes of silk. With them are a bull and a fish that bring them each day something new they did not bring the day before. The fish swims in the rivers of Paradise eating every flavor of the rivers; in the evening the bull gores it with its horn and it is slaughtered, so they eat its flesh and taste in it every flavor of the rivers of Paradise. The bull spends the night grazing in Paradise eating its fruits; in the morning the fish charges it and slaughters it with its tail, so they eat its flesh and taste in it every fruit of Paradise. They gaze upon their dwellings and call upon Allah for the Hour to rise."
"9541","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The martyrs dwell upon Bariq, a river at the gate of Paradise, in a green dome. Their provision comes out to them from Paradise morning and evening.""**"
"9542","Abd Allah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: the souls of the martyrs are in the bellies of green birds that roam freely in Paradise wherever they wish, then return to lamps suspended from the Throne."
"9543","Salim al-Aftas reported: when Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, Musab ibn Umayr, and Abd Allah ibn Jahsh, may Allah be pleased with them, were struck down, and they saw the goodness and provision granted them, their longing for martyrdom only grew. They wished their companions could know the goodness and provision that befell them. Allah said: I shall convey it from you on their behalf. So Allah sent down: Think not of those killed in the path of Allah as dead."
"9544","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""No soul dies while it has goodness from Allah and wishes to return to you, even if it had the bliss of the world and all in it, except being killed in the path of Allah. He loves to return and be killed again for the reward from Allah he sees.""**"
"9545","Rashid ibn Hubaysh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to Ubadah ibn al-Samit to visit him in his illness and said: > **""Do you know who the martyr of my nation is?""** The people fell silent. Ubadah said, Prop me up, so they propped him, and he said, O Messenger of Allah, the patient one seeking reward. The Messenger of Allah said: > **""Then the martyrs of my nation are few. Being killed in the path of Allah is martyrdom, plague is martyrdom, drowning is martyrdom, the belly is martyrdom, and the woman in childbed: her child drags her with its cord to Paradise.""**"
"9546","The same report of Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, was narrated with his chain, mentioning the same account, except that in its chain is a man who was not named."
"9547","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to visit me while I was ill, with some of the Helpers. He said: > **""Do you know who the martyr is?""** They fell silent. I said to my wife, prop me up, so she propped me, and I said, Whoever accepts Islam, then emigrates, then is killed in the path of Allah, he is a martyr. The Prophet said: > **""If the martyrs of my nation are only these, then they are few. Being killed in the path of Allah is martyrdom, the drowned is a martyr, the one dead of the belly is a martyr, plague is martyrdom, and the woman in childbed: her child drags her with its cord to Paradise.""**"
"9548","Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: we visited Abd Allah ibn Rawahah to cheer his illness, and he lost consciousness. We said, May Allah have mercy on you; we had hoped you would die otherwise, and we had hoped martyrdom for you. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered while we spoke of this and said: > **""What do you count as martyrdom?""** The people fell silent. Abd Allah stirred and said, Will you not answer the Messenger of Allah? Then he himself answered: We count martyrdom as being killed. He said: > **""Then the martyrs of my nation are few. In being killed there is martyrdom, in plague there is martyrdom, in the belly there is martyrdom, in drowning there is martyrdom, and in the woman in childbed whose child causes her death altogether there is martyrdom.""**"
"9549","Rabi al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, visited my nephew Jabr al-Ansari, and his family began weeping over him. Jabr said to them, Do not harm the Messenger of Allah with your voices. The Messenger of Allah said: > **""Let them weep while he is alive; when the soul becomes due, let them be silent.""** One of those present said, We never thought you would die upon your bed; we expected you to be killed in the path of Allah with the Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah said: > **""Is martyrdom only being killed in the path of Allah? Then the martyrs of my nation are few. The stabbed is martyrdom, the belly is martyrdom, plague and the woman dying in childbed altogether are martyrdom, burning is martyrdom, drowning and collapse are martyrdom, and pleurisy is martyrdom.""**"
"9550","Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to us and said: > **""Whom do you count as martyr among you?""** We said, Whoever is killed in the path of Allah. He said: > **""Whoever is killed in the path of Allah is a martyr. Whoever drowns in the path of Allah is a martyr. Whoever the belly kills is a martyr. And the woman whom her childbed kills is a martyr.""**"
"9551","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported: one day the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to his Companions: > **""Whom do you count as martyrs among you?""** They said, Whoever is killed in the path of Allah, patient, seeking reward, advancing and not retreating, is a martyr. He said: > **""Then the martyrs of my nation are few. The one killed in the path of Allah is a martyr, the man who dies upon his bed in the path of Allah is a martyr, the one dead of the belly is a martyr, the stung is a martyr, the drowned is a martyr, the choked is a martyr, the one devoured by a beast is a martyr, the one thrown from his mount is a martyr, the one under rubble is a martyr, the one dead of pleurisy is a martyr, and the woman in childbed: her child drags her with its cord to Paradise.""**"
"9552","Sad, meaning ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""You count as martyrdom being killed, plague, drowning, the belly, and the death of a woman in childbed altogether, her death in her confinement.""**"
"9553","Abd Allah ibn Busr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, visited Sad ibn Ubadah and said three times: > **""Whom do you count as martyrs of my nation?""** They said, Allah and His Messenger know best. Sad ibn Ubadah said, If the Messenger of Allah permits me, I shall tell him who the martyrs of his nation are. He said: > **""Then tell me who the martyrs of my nation are.""** He said, Prop me up, so they propped him. He said, Whoever believes in Allah, strives in the path of Allah, and fights till he is killed, he is a martyr. He said: > **""Then the martyrs of my nation are few. Being killed in the path of Allah is martyrdom, the one dead of the belly is a martyr, the stabbed is a martyr, the drowned is a martyr, and the woman in childbed is a martyr.""**"
"9554","Abd al-Malik ibn Harun, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: one day the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Whom do you count as martyr among you?""** We said, O Messenger of Allah, whoever is killed in the path of Allah. He said: > **""Then the martyrs of my nation are few. Whoever is killed in the path of Allah is a martyr, the belly is a martyr, the one who falls to death is a martyr, the woman in childbed is a martyr, and the drowned is a martyr.""** Al-Hulwani added: consumption is martyrdom, burning is martyrdom, and the stranger dying far from home is a martyr."
"9555","Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: he brought the charity to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, three times, and he asked: > **""Whom do you count as martyr among you?""** They said, The one killed in the path of Allah. He said: > **""Then the martyrs of my nation are few. Being killed in the path of Allah is martyrdom, plague is martyrdom, the woman in childbed is martyrdom, burning is martyrdom, drowning is martyrdom, consumption is martyrdom, and the belly is martyrdom.""**"
"9556","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, raised it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: > **""The belly and drowning are martyrdom.""**"
"9557","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported: she said, O Messenger of Allah, is the martyr only the one killed in the path of Allah? He said: > **""O Aishah, then the martyrs of my nation are few. Whoever says twenty-five times a day: O Allah, bless death and what is after death, then dies upon his bed, Allah grants him the reward of a martyr.""**"
"9558","Uqbah ibn Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Whoever is thrown from his mount and dies is a martyr.""**"
"9559","Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: whoever falls from the mountain peaks, or is devoured by beasts, or drowns in the seas, is a martyr before Allah."
"9560","Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani reported: martyrs were mentioned before Abu Inabah al-Khawlani, and the dead of plague, the dead of the belly, and the woman in childbed were mentioned. Abu Inabah grew angry and said: the Companions of our Prophet narrated to us from our Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: > **""The witnesses of Allah upon the earth are the trustees of Allah over His creation, whether they are killed or die.""**"
"9561","Ibrahim ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaah reported that Abu Muhammad, one of the students of Ibn Masud, narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: > **""Most of the martyrs of my nation are the owners of beds. Many a slain one between two ranks: Allah knows best his intent.""**"
"9562","Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Allah will surely aid this religion through peoples who have no share in it.""**"
"9563","Maymun ibn Sunbad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""The uprightness of my nation lies with its worst ones.""**"
"9564","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Allah, blessed and exalted, aids this religion through peoples who have no share in it.""**"
"9565","Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: had I not heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""Allah will surely give benefit to this religion through Christians of Rabiah upon the shore of the Euphrates.""** I would not have left a bedouin but I would have killed him or he would accept Islam."
"9566","Abu Musa al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: a chapter nearly the length of Barah was sent down, then it was lifted, and I remembered from it that Allah aids this religion through peoples who have no share in it."
"9567","Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Allah will surely aid this religion through men who are not of its folk.""**"
"9568","Al-Numan ibn Amr ibn Muqarrin, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Allah surely aids this religion through the wicked man.""**"
"9569","Abd Allah, meaning ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah the Exalted surely aids this religion through the wicked man."
"9570","Khubayb ibn Yasaf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: he came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he intended a campaign, he and a man of his people, and they had not accepted Islam. They said, We are ashamed that our people witness a battle we do not witness with them. He said: > **""Have you two accepted Islam?""** They said, No. He said: > **""We seek no aid of polytheists against polytheists.""** So they accepted Islam and witnessed the battle with him. Khubayb killed a man who had struck him a blow, then married his daughter after that. She used to say, May you never lack a man who adorned you with this sword-belt of a wound. And he would say, May you never lack a man who hastened your father to the Fire."
"9571","Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, set out on the day of Uhud until he passed Thaniyyat al-Wada, and there was a rugged battalion. He said: > **""Who are these?""** They said, Abd Allah ibn Ubayy with six hundred of his clients, Jews of Qaynuqa. He said: > **""Have they accepted Islam?""** They said, No, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""Order them to return, for we seek no aid of polytheists against polytheists.""**"
"9572","A man among the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, reported: he heard the Messenger of Allah say: > **""Leave the Abyssinians while they leave you, for none shall extract the treasure of the Kabah but the one with thin shanks from among the Abyssinians.""**"
"9573","Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported: he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""Leave the Turks while they leave you.""**"
"9574","Muawiyah ibn Khudayj, may Allah be pleased with him, reported: he was with Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan when the letter of his governor reached him, telling that he had fallen upon the Turks, routed them, killed many of them, and taken much spoils. Muawiyah grew angry at that, then ordered it written back to him: I have understood what you mentioned of killing and spoils, but let me never learn that you returned to any of that, nor fight them until my order reaches you. Muawiyah ibn Khudayj asked him, Why, Commander of the Faithful? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""The Turks shall surely overpower the Arabs until they drive them back to the pastures of wormwood and qaysum.""** So I dislike fighting them for that reason."
"9575","Ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the Muslims to leave the Turks alone so long as they leave the Muslims alone. He then warned that the first people to strip his nation of the dominion and bounty which Allah (Glorified and Exalted is He) granted them would be the sons of Qantura. > **""Leave the Turks as long as they leave you, for the first who will strip my nation of its kingdom and what Allah has granted them are the sons of Qantura.""**"
"9576","Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) foretold that Allah would fill the hands of the Muslims with non-Arabs. They would stand firm like lions, never fleeing, striking the necks of the Muslims and consuming their war booty. > **""Allah will fill your hands with non-Arabs, and they will stand firm like lions who do not flee; they will strike your necks and consume your fay.""**"
"9577","Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade wishing to encounter the enemy in battle. The reason given is that a person does not know what will result from such an encounter. > **""Do not wish to meet the enemy, for you do not know what will come of that.""**"
"9578","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never fought any people until he had first invited them to Islam. This was his constant practice before any military engagement."
"9579","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) to fight a people, then sent a man after him with instructions not to call him back from behind but to tell him plainly that he must not fight them until he has invited them to Islam. > **""Do not fight them until you have invited them.""**"
"9580","Marthad ibn Zabyan (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that a letter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached his people, and none could read it aloud until a man of Banu Dubay'ah read it. It was addressed from the Messenger of Allah to Bakr ibn Wail with a brief call to embrace Islam and be safe. > **""From the Messenger of Allah to Bakr ibn Wail: accept Islam and you will be safe.""**"
"9581","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Bakr ibn Wail commanding them to accept Islam and be safe. None was found to read it to them except a man of Banu Dubay'ah, for which reason those people came to be called Banu al-Katib, the sons of the scribe. > **""Accept Islam and you will be safe.""**"
"9582","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that a servant will be with those whom he loves. He further reports that before his passing, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote letters calling to Islam to Khosrow, to Caesar, and to every mighty ruler. > **""A servant is with those whom he loves.""**"
"9583","Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man to accept Islam and be safe. The man replied that he found himself reluctant, whereupon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated the command even in that state. > **""Accept Islam and you will be safe. Accept, even if you are reluctant.""**"
"9584","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited a sick man of Banu al-Najjar and said to him, O maternal uncle, say there is no god but Allah. The man asked whether he was a paternal or maternal uncle, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, rather a maternal uncle, and repeated the call. The man asked if it would be good for him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said yes. > **""O maternal uncle, say: there is no god but Allah. Yes, it is good for you.""**"
"9585","Asma bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with her and her father) reports that on the day of the Conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her father Abu Quhafah (may Allah be pleased with him) to accept Islam and be safe. > **""Accept Islam and you will be safe.""**"
"9586","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to his Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) and said that Allah had sent him as a mercy to all people, asking them to convey from him and not to differ as the disciples differed over Jesus (peace be upon him), when those near accepted and those far disliked it until Allah made each speak the tongue of his mission. The Companions replied that they would convey, so he sent Abdullah ibn Hudhafah to Khosrow, Sulayt ibn Amr to Hawdhah of Yamamah, Ala ibn al-Hadrami to al-Mundhir of Hajar, Amr ibn al-As to Jayfar and Abbad of Oman, Dihyah al-Kalbi to Caesar, Shuja ibn Wahb to al-Mundhir al-Ghassani, and Amr ibn Umayyah to the Negus. All returned before the Prophet's death except Ala, who was in Bahrain when he passed away. > **""Allah has sent me as a mercy to all people, so convey from me, may Allah have mercy on you, and do not differ as the disciples differed over Jesus.""**"
"9587","Dihyah al-Kalbi (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with a letter to Caesar of Rome. After seeking entry as the envoy of the Messenger of Allah, he was admitted among the bishops and handed the letter, which opened in the name of Allah and was addressed from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah to Caesar of Rome. Caesar's red, blue-eyed nephew objected that the writer began with himself and wrote chief rather than king, but the letter was read to the end. Caesar then questioned Dihyah privately and summoned the chief bishop, who declared this was the one foretold by Moses and Jesus whom they awaited, and that he himself believed and would follow. Caesar replied that he knew it was so, but that he could not act lest he lose his kingdom and the Romans kill him. > **""In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Caesar, the chief of Rome.""**"
"9588","Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ask thrice who would carry his letter to the tyrant of Rome, then promised Paradise to whoever would go. An Ansari named Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Khaliq volunteered, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assured him Paradise whether he delivered it, was killed, or perished. The envoy announced himself as the envoy of the Messenger of Allah, was admitted, and the tyrant recognized he had brought truth from a commissioned Prophet. After the Romans gathered, rejected the letter, and killed one man among them who believed, the envoy returned with the news, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of the slain believer that Allah would raise him on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone. > **""Who will take my letter to the tyrant of Rome? Whoever goes shall have Paradise. Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone.""**"
"9589","Abdullah ibn Shaddad reports that Abu Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) said the first day he felt awe of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was when Caesar, in his kingdom and court, said that had he known him he would have walked to him to kiss his head and wash his feet, while sweat poured from his brow onto the Prophet's letter. Abu Sufyan remained in awe until he embraced Islam. The letter called the People of the Book to the common word that they worship none but Allah and take none as lords besides Him, declared that Allah sent His Messenger with guidance and the true religion to prevail over all religion, and commanded fighting those of the Book who disbelieve until they pay the tax in humiliation. > **""O People of the Book, come to a word common between us and you: that we worship none but Allah and associate nothing with Him.""**"
"9590","Khalid ibn Said (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen with orders that whenever among the Arabs he heard the call to prayer, he was not to molest them, but those among whom he did not hear it he was to invite to Islam, and if they refused, to strive against them. > **""Whomever among the Arabs you meet and hear the call to prayer among them, do not molest them; those among whom you do not hear it, invite them to Islam, and if they refuse, then strive against them.""**"
"9591","Dihyah al-Kalbi (may Allah be pleased with him) reports at length that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Caesar, where he delivered a letter addressed from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah to Heraclius of Rome. Caesar's red, blue-eyed nephew with straight hair objected that the writer began with himself and wrote chief of Rome rather than king, but Caesar ordered the letter read fully, dismissed the court, admitted Dihyah privately, and consulted the chief bishop, who affirmed this was the awaited one foretold by Jesus and that he himself believed and would follow, while Caesar said doing so would cost his kingdom. Caesar then summoned Abu Sufyan, who was present in his land, and questioned him about the man risen among them: a youth of unmatched lineage, never known to lie, whose followers never return to disbelief while deserters return to him, and who meets victory and defeat in battle. To each answer Caesar said, this is a sign of prophethood, and he charged Dihyah to convey that he knew him to be a Prophet but would not abandon his kingdom. > **""From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, the chief of Rome. Convey to your master that I know he is a Prophet, but I will not abandon my kingdom.""**"
"9592","Umayr ibn Muqbil al-Judhami reports from his father that Rifa'ah ibn Zayd al-Judhami came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who wrote for him a charter in the name of Allah addressed to Rifa'ah, sending him to his people and all who joined them to call them to Allah and His Messenger, so that believers would be in the party of Allah and His Messenger while those who turned away would have two months of security. His people accepted, and he encamped at the lava field until Dihyah al-Kalbi passed returning from Caesar with trade goods at a valley called Shanar, where al-Hunayd ibn Uways attacked and seized all he had. Men of Rifa'ah's people, among them Numan ibn Abi Jial, pursued and fought; Qurrah ibn Ashqar struck Numan's ankle and cried out his clan name, and Numan struck his knee in return. Hassan ibn Millah, who had accompanied Dihyah earlier and taught him the Opening Chapter, helped recover what was taken and return it to Dihyah. Dihyah then told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought the blood of al-Hunayd and his father, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Zayd ibn Harithah with an army as Ghatfan, Judham, and others mobilized. > **""In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: this is a writ from Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Rifa'ah ibn Zayd. Whoever believes is in the party of Allah and His Messenger, and whoever turns away has security for two months.""**"
"9593","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to a tribe of the Arabs inviting them to Islam, but they rejected the letter. When the messengers returned, he said that had he sent it to the Azd of Shanuah and Aslam on the coast of Oman they would have accepted it. He then wrote to al-Julanda inviting him to Islam, who accepted, embraced Islam, and sent a gift which arrived after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had passed away, whereupon Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) treated it as inheritance and divided it between Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) and the people. > **""Had I sent it to a people of the Azd of Shanuah and Aslam on the shore of Oman, they would have accepted it.""**"
"9594","Mujammi ibn Attab ibn Shamir reports from his father that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he had an aged father and brothers and asked leave to go to them hoping they would accept Islam and that he might bring them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied that if they accepted it would be better for them, and if they remained where they were, Islam is still broad and spacious. > **""If they accept Islam it is better for them, and if they remain, Islam is broad and spacious.""**"
"9595","Abu Wail reports that Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to the people of Persia inviting them to Islam. The letter opened in the name of Allah, from Khalid to Rusaym, Mihran, and the chiefs of Persia, with peace upon whoever follows guidance, calling them to Islam, then to pay the tax in humiliation if they refused, and warning that with him were men who loved being slain in the path of Allah as the Persians loved wine, closing again with peace upon whoever follows guidance."
"9596","Al-Jarud reports that he copied this document from the copy of Ala ibn al-Hadrami (may Allah be pleased with him), which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had written when he sent him to Bahrain. It opens in the name of Allah from Muhammad, the unlettered Qurayshi Hashimi Prophet, the Messenger and Prophet of Allah to all creation, as a covenant to Ala and the Muslims with him: to fear Allah as much as able, for Ala is appointed over them and ordered to fear Allah alone without partner, to be gentle, to conduct himself well, and to judge between them and all he meets by the justice of Allah's Book. They are ordered to obey him when he acts justly in judgment and division and shows mercy when asked, to hear, obey, and aid him well, for obedience to him is a mighty right they cannot fully discharge, just as obedience to Allah and His Messenger and fidelity to their covenant is a right upon all people, and likewise the Muslims owe their rulers a due right of obedience, since obedience attains good and delivers from evil. The Prophet calls Allah to witness over every governor of Muslim affairs, small or great, to seek Allah's choice and appoint the most excellent, and provides that if death befalls Ala, then Khalid ibn al-Walid, the sword of Allah, succeeds him, whom they must likewise hear and obey and aid. > **""In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: fear Allah, O Muslims, as much as you are able. Hear and obey Ala ibn al-Hadrami when he judges with justice, and if death befalls him, then Khalid ibn al-Walid succeeds him.""**"
"9597","Abu Wail reports that Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), when Ibn al-Nawwahah was slain, said that he and Ibn Uthal had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as envoys of Musaylimah the liar. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked whether they testified that he was the Messenger of Allah, and they replied that they testified Musaylimah was the messenger of Allah, whereupon he said that had he ever killed a delegation he would have struck their necks. Thus the practice ran that envoys are not killed. As for Ibn Uthal, Allah sufficed against him, while this man remained until Allah enabled the Muslims over him. > **""Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? Had I ever killed a delegation, I would have struck your necks.""**"
"9598","Ibn Muayz al-Sa'di reports that he went out before dawn to water his horse, passed the mosque of Banu Hanifah while they were saying Musaylimah was the messenger of Allah, and informed Abdullah ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him), who sent the police to bring them, called them to repent, accepted their repentance, and released them, but had Abdullah ibn al-Nawwahah beheaded. When asked why he killed one and left the rest in the same affair, he said he had heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) question this man and Ibn Uthal ibn Hajar when they came as envoys, and when they countered by asking him to testify for Musaylimah, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared faith in Allah and His messengers and said that had he killed envoys he would have killed them, and for that reason Ibn Masud killed him. > **""I believe in Allah and His messengers; had I ever killed a delegation, I would have killed you both.""**"
"9599","Nuaym ibn Masud (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the two envoys of Musaylimah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said that were it not that envoys are not killed he would have struck their necks. He sent with them a letter from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah to Musaylimah the liar declaring that the earth belongs to Allah, who grants it to whom He wills among His servants, and that the good end is for the righteous. He also said that the Hour will not come until thirty liars emerge, each claiming to be a Prophet. > **""Were it not that envoys are not to be killed, I would have struck your necks. The earth belongs to Allah; He grants it to whom He wills, and the good end is for the righteous. The Hour will not come until thirty liars emerge, each claiming to be a Prophet.""**"
"9600","Wabr ibn Mushir reports that Musaylimah sent him with Ibn Shalghaf and Ibn al-Nawwahah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). His two elder companions spoke first, testifying that the Prophet was a prophet and that Musaylimah would come after him. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked the youth what he said, Wabr replied that he affirmed whatever the Prophet affirmed and denied whatever he denied. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared his witness, by the number of grains of sand in the desert, that Musaylimah was a liar, ordered the two men seized and held in the house of Kaysan, then released them after intercession from one of his Companions. > **""I testify, by the number of grains of sand in the desert, that Musaylimah is a liar.""**"
"9601","Ibn Kab ibn Malik reports from his paternal uncle (may Allah be pleased with them) that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him against Ibn Abi al-Huqayq at Khaybar, he forbade the killing of women and children."
"9602","Ayyub reports that he heard a man of his people narrating from his father that he accompanied an expedition sent by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in which they were forbidden to kill hired laborers, the very aged, and household servants."
"9603","Sab ibn Jaththamah al-Laythi (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the children of the polytheists, and he first answered that they be slain along with their parents. The narrator adds that on the day of Khaybar the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that, and in another wording the question concerned a raiding party striking offspring in the fierceness of a night attack. > **""Slay them along with them.""**"
"9604","Kab ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the killing of women and small children."
"9605","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that a man seized a captive woman, she struggled with him over his sword hilt, and he killed her. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by her and was told of her case, he forbade the killing of women. In another wording, he passed a slain woman on the day of the Trench, asked who killed her, a man admitted it saying she wrestled his sword from him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent."
"9606","Awf ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that women are not to be killed. > **""Do not kill women.""**"
"9607","Abdullah ibn Atik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him and his companions to kill Ibn Abi al-Huqayq at Khaybar, he forbade the killing of women and children."
"9608","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about Muslim cavalry falling upon polytheists and killing them along with their sons, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied that those children are with their fathers. > **""They are with their fathers.""**"
"9609","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the killing of women and young boys."
"9610","Aswad ibn Sari (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), campaigned with him, and saw victory, but on that day people killed even infants. When a man objected that they were only children of polytheists, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied that the best of people are the children of polytheists, then thrice warned never to kill offspring, explaining that every soul is born upon natural disposition until its tongue expresses itself, after which its parents make it Jewish or Christian. In another wording, when the killing of offspring reached him, he asked what carried people beyond bounds in killing. > **""Do not kill offspring, do not kill offspring. Every soul is born upon natural disposition until its tongue expresses itself, then its parents make it Jewish or Christian.""**"
"9611","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dispatched armies, he commanded them to go forth in the name of Allah, fighting in the path of Allah those who disbelieved, without treachery, theft from spoils, mutilation, or killing of infants and cloistered monks. In another wording he added neither infant, woman, nor aged elder. > **""Go forth in the name of Allah. Fight in the path of Allah those who disbelieved in Allah. Do not act treacherously, do not steal from spoils, do not mutilate, and do not kill infants or cloistered monks.""**"
"9612","Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that whoever kills a young or old person, burns a palm, cuts a fruit-bearing tree, or slaughters a sheep merely for its hide will not return even with his account balanced. > **""Whoever kills a young or old person, burns a palm, cuts a fruit-bearing tree, or slaughters a sheep for its hide will not return even balanced.""**"
"9613","Jarir ibn Abdullah al-Bajali (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment, he commanded them in the name of Allah, in the path of Allah, upon the creed of the Messenger of Allah, without theft from spoils, treachery, mutilation, or killing of infants. > **""In the name of Allah, in the path of Allah, upon the creed of the Messenger of Allah: do not steal from spoils, do not act treacherously, do not mutilate, and do not kill infants.""**"
"9614","Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment, he commanded them to raid in the name of Allah, fight those who disbelieved in Allah, and avoid mutilation, theft from spoils, treachery, and the killing of infants and aged elders. > **""Raid in the name of Allah and fight those who disbelieved in Allah. Do not mutilate, do not steal from spoils, do not act treacherously, and do not kill an infant or an aged elder.""**"
"9615","Ata ibn Abi Rabah reports that he was with Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) when a Basran youth asked him a question, and Ibn Umar recounted that he had been one of ten in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, Ali, Ibn Masud, Hudhayfah, Abu Said al-Khudri, another named man, and himself (may Allah be pleased with them). An Ansari youth greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked which believers were most excellent and most sagacious; he answered the best in character, and those who remember death most and prepare for it before it descends. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then warned that indecency brings plague and new pains, short measure brings drought, hardship, and tyranny, withholding charity withholds rain save for the sake of beasts, breaking the covenant of Allah and His Messenger empowers enemies, and rulers who abandon the Book bring strife among their people. He then ordered Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) to ready a detachment, fixed his black turban leaving four fingers behind, praised Allah, handed him the banner, and charged all to fight disbelievers without theft, treachery, mutilation, or killing infants, declaring this the covenant and practice of the Messenger of Allah. > **""The best in character. Those who remember death most and prepare for it most. Fight together in the path of Allah: fight those who disbelieved in Allah, do not steal from spoils, do not act treacherously, do not mutilate, and do not kill an infant.""**"
"9616","Abu Thalabah al-Khushani (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the killing of women and small children."
"9617","Abu Said (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the killing of women and boys, saying that such captives belong to whoever prevails. > **""They belong to whoever prevails.""**"
"9618","Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that nothing informed of its kind surpasses a thousand of its like except man. > **""Nothing informed of its kind surpasses a thousand of its like except man.""**"
"9619","Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that he found among beasts one mount better than a hundred, and among men one man better than a hundred. > **""One mount is better than a hundred, and one man is better than a hundred.""**"
"9620","Samurah ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that he found no class of beasts in which one was better than a hundred of its fellows, except man, among whom one may be found better than a hundred men. > **""One man may be found better than a hundred men.""**"
"9621","Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar reports from his father that the mother of Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him), widowed and beautiful, came to Madinah and refused marriage except to one who would maintain her son Samurah until maturity; an Ansari married her on that condition. Each year the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inspected Ansari youths and dispatched those who had reached maturity. One year he passed a youth into service but held Samurah back, whereupon Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) protested that a younger lad had been passed while he, who could throw him, was held back. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told him to wrestle him, Samurah threw him, and was passed for the expedition. > **""Wrestle him then.""**"
"9622","Rafi ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that he and his uncle came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he set out for Badr, and he asked to go along. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clenched his hand saying he deemed him too young and unsure of his conduct against the foe. When Rafi said he was the best of archers, the Prophet still sent him back, so he did not witness Badr. > **""I deem you too young, and I do not know what you would do when you meet the foe.""**"
"9623","Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to consult in war, and enjoined him to do likewise."
"9624","Muhammad ibn Salam al-Bikandi reports that Amr ibn Madikarib, famed in pre-Islamic battles, embraced Islam, came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and was later directed by Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) to Sad ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) at Qadisiyyah, where he fought valiantly. Umar wrote to Sad that he had reinforced him with two thousand men in the persons of Amr ibn Madikarib and Talhah ibn Khuwaylid al-Asadi, instructing him to consult them in war but grant them no command."
"9625","Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to the campaign of Dhat al-Salasil, he forbade the men to kindle fire for three nights. The men asked Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to intercede, but Amr warned that whoever kindled fire would be cast into it. They then met the enemy, routed them, and Amr forbade pursuit. When they complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Amr explained they were few, he feared pursuit would expose them and that enemy reserves would turn upon them, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praised his decision."
"9626","In another wording, Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) said he forbade them to kindle fire fearing the enemy would see how few they were."
"9627","Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9628","Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9629","Musayyab ibn Najabah reports that he entered upon Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him and his father), who said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9630","Husayn ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9631","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9632","Nubayt ibn Sharit (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9633","Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9634","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a Companion to kill a Jewish man, and when the Companion asked leave to say whatever the mission required, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told him to say whatever occurred to him, for war is deception. > **""Say whatever occurs to you, for war is but deception.""**"
"9635","Awf ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9636","Nawwas ibn Siman (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that war is deception. > **""War is deception.""**"
"9637","Amr ibn Umayyah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him alone as a scout toward Quraysh. He came to the wooden frame of Khubayb (may Allah be pleased with him), fearing watchers, climbed it, freed Khubayb, and the body fell to the ground. He withdrew a short distance, turned, and found no trace of Khubayb, as though the earth had swallowed him, and no trace has been seen to this hour."
"9638","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) and Buraydah (may Allah be pleased with him) report that the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was black and his standard white."
"9639","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was black and his standard white, with inscribed upon it that there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."
"9640","Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was black."
"9641","Mazidah al-Abdi (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tied the banners of the Helpers and made them yellow."
"9642","Kurayz ibn Samah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tied a red banner for Banu Sulaym."
"9643","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the banner of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained with Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), the banner of the Helpers with Sad ibn Ubadah (may Allah be pleased with him), and when fighting grew fierce the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take shelter under the banner of the Helpers."
"9644","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) bore the banner of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Badr, Qays ibn Sad bore the banner of Ali, and Ali bore the banner of the Emigrants in every encounter."
"9645","Muharib reports that Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Ziyad that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that an enemy cannot prevail over a people whose standard is borne by a man of Banu Bakr ibn Wail. > **""An enemy cannot prevail over a people whose standard is with a man of Banu Bakr ibn Wail.""**"
"9646","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at the watering place when a woman brought her son saying he wished to go forth to battle while she forbade him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told the son not to leave his mother without her permission or until death parted them, for that would be greater in reward. > **""Do not leave your mother until she permits you or death takes her, for that is greater for your reward.""**"
"9647","Abdullah ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that a man and his mother came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he sought to join the struggle and his mother forbade him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told him to remain with his mother, for his reward with her equals his reward in struggle. > **""Remain with your mother, for your reward with her is like your reward in struggle.""**"
"9648","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that even if the campaign stood at the door of the house, one should not go except with the permission of both parents. > **""Even if the campaign stands at the door of the house, do not go except with the permission of your parents.""**"
"9649","Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him and his father) reports that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying he wished to pledge allegiance for struggle. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked whether his parents were alive; when he said yes, he commanded him to strive in serving them. > **""Are your parents alive? Then strive in serving them.""**"
"9650","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, ordered preparation against Khaybar and barred weak mounts. Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, sought gear from his mother, who pleaded with him by her nursing. She complained secretly to the Prophet, who told her to return, for it was sufficed. When Abu Hurayrah came, the Prophet turned away and explained service to parents is itself in Allah's path, so Abu Hurayrah stayed from campaigns two years until his mother died. > **""Prepare for this unjust town, for Allah will open it for you if Allah wills.""** > **""Go back, for you have been sufficed.""** > **""Does one of you think that being with his parents means he is not in Allah's path? Rather he is in Allah's path when he honors them and fulfils their right.""**"
"9651","Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, sent a detachment, was joined by a man demanding a share of booty, then a fixed three dinars. After booty was taken, the Prophet, peace be upon him, was asked about him. > **""For him in this world and the Hereafter there is nothing except these three dinars he took.""**"
"9652","Maymunah bint Sad, may Allah be pleased with her, asked about one who does not fight but gives wealth for fighting: whose reward? The Prophet, peace be upon him, divided the reward. > **""For him is the reward of his wealth, and for the one who goes is the reward of what he intended thereby.""**"
"9653","Umm Kabshah of Udhrah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked leave to join an army, not to fight but to tend wounded and sick and give water. The Prophet, peace be upon him, refused. > **""No.""** > **""Were it not that it would become a precedent and it would be said that so-and-so woman went out, I would permit you, but stay.""**"
"9654","Layla al-Ghifariyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported she used to go out with the Messenger, peace be upon him, to treat the wounded. Companion report, prose only."
"9655","Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, used to take Ansari women on campaigns to give the sick water and treat the wounded. Companion report, prose only."
"9656","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her, asked to join battle. The Prophet, peace be upon him, said jihad was not prescribed for women, but permitted nursing service. > **""O Umm Sulaym, jihad has not been prescribed for women.""** > **""Then yes.""**"
"9657","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, joined campaigning, fasting, and travel with gain, health, and sufficiency. > **""Fight and you will gain booty, fast and you will be healthy, travel and you will be enriched.""**"
"9658","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, commended travel for health and safety. > **""Travel, you will be healthy and safe.""**"
"9659","Isam al-Muzani, a Companion, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, ordered detachments to kill no one where they saw a mosque or heard a caller to prayer. His squad met a driver of women in Tihamah, offered Islam, granted delay, heard verses of parting love, then the man submitted to execution and the woman died grieving over him. > **""When you see a mosque or hear a caller to prayer, kill no one.""**"
"9660","Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported among the last words of the Messenger, peace be upon him, was expulsion of Jews of Hijaz and Najran from Arabia, and warning against taking graves of prophets as mosques. > **""Expel the Jews of Hijaz and Najran from the Arabian Peninsula, and know that the worst of people are those who take the graves of their prophets as mosques.""**"
"9661","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported the last charge of the Messenger, peace be upon him, was that two religions shall not remain in Arabia. > **""Two religions shall not remain in the Arabian Peninsula.""**"
"9662","Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, ordered that no religion besides Islam be left in Madinah except it be removed. Companion report with command, prose and speech. > **""Leave no religion in Madinah besides Islam except it be removed.""**"
"9663","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, ordered expulsion of the Jews from Arabia. > **""Expel the Jews from the Arabian Peninsula.""**"
"9664","Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger, peace be upon him, foretell conquest of the places where wormwood grows. > **""You will conquer the places where wormwood grows.""**"
"9665","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, loved to advance against the enemy after the sun declined. Companion report, prose only."
"9666","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, if he had not met the enemy in the morning, delayed until winds blew and prayer times came, supplicating for strength from Allah. > **""O Allah, by You I charge and by You I fight, and there is no power nor strength except with Allah the High, the Mighty.""**"
"9667","Utbah ibn Ghazwan al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported they fought with the Messenger, peace be upon him, and when the sun declined he ordered the charge. > **""Charge.""**"
"9668","Makhariq met Ammar on the Day of the Camel and asked about fighting with him; Ammar told him to fight under his own people's banner, for the Messenger, peace be upon him, preferred a man fight under his people's banner. Companion ruling, prose only."
"9669","Aslam Abu Imran heard Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, say they formed ranks at Badr, one rushed forward, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, called them back. > **""With me, with me.""**"
"9670","Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reported with the Prophet, peace be upon him, each man guarded his brother's knee in the rank as carefully as an arrow, seeking martyrdom alike, as a compact wall. Companion description, prose only."
"9671","Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said standing in rank in Allah's path surpasses sixty years of worship. > **""A man's standing in the rank in Allah's path is better than his worship for sixty years.""**"
"9672","Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the battle cry of the Prophet, peace be upon him, was an invocation of all good. > **""O Source of all good.""**"
"9673","Utbah ibn Farqad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, seeing his Companions fall back, called out to them by the Chapter of the Cow. > **""O Companions of the Chapter of the Cow.""**"
"9674","Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj from his father from his grandfather reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, at Badr asked how they would fight; Asim ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, answered: arrows at arrow-range, stones with three stones at stone-range, spears at spear-range, then swords. The Prophet approved it as the way of war. > **""How will you fight the people when you meet them?""** > **""Thus war was ordained; whoever fights, let him fight as Asim fights.""**"
"9675","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, said the best companions are four, best detachments four hundred, best armies four thousand, and twelve thousand are never defeated by fewness if truthful and patient. > **""The best of companions are four, the best of detachments are four hundred, the best of armies are four thousand, and no people of twelve thousand are defeated by fewness if they are truthful and patient.""**"
"9676","Abu Ayyub Khalid ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said whoever meets the enemy and endures until killed or victorious is spared trial in the grave. > **""Whoever meets the enemy and endures until he is killed or prevails will not be tried in his grave.""**"
"9677","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, said flight from two is flight, but flight from three is not flight. > **""Whoever flees from two has fled, and whoever flees from three has not fled.""**"
"9678","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, used to urge his Companions to single combat. Companion report, prose only."
"9679","Abu Ishaq asked al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, whether one who charges the polytheists alone casts himself to ruin; he said no, citing the command to fight in Allah's path, and said the ruin verse concerns spending. Companion ruling, prose only."
"9680","Abu Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported with the Prophet, peace be upon him, on a raid he heard him recite the Master of the Day of Judgment, You we worship and You we seek help, and saw men felled with angels striking before and behind them. > **""Master of the Day of Judgment, You we worship and You we seek help.""**"
"9681","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, reported at Khaybar a man was sent and showed cowardice, and Muhammad ibn Maslamah spoke; the Prophet, peace be upon him, forbade wishing for battle, commanded asking well-being, taught a supplication, sitting firm, then rising with takbir. > **""Do not wish to meet the enemy, and ask Allah for well-being, for you do not know what trial you will face from them; when you meet them say: O Allah, You are our Lord and their Lord, our forelocks and theirs are in Your hand, and only You kill them; then keep to the ground sitting, and when they close in, rise and magnify Allah.""**"
"9682","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said Allah aids Muslims through the supplication of the oppressed. > **""Allah aids the Muslims only through the supplication of the oppressed among them.""**"
"9683","Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, burned some property of Banu Nadir. Companion report, prose only."
"9684","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported a Muslim granted a man protection while Abu Ubaydah commanded; Khalid and Amr objected, but Abu Ubaydah upheld it, having heard the Prophet, peace be upon him. > **""One of the Muslims grants protection on behalf of the Muslims.""**"
"9685","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger, peace be upon him, say some Muslims grant protection on behalf of all. > **""Some of the Muslims grant protection on behalf of the Muslims.""**"
"9686","A man of Egypt from Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, about captive Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr: Amr wished him to claim safety, citing the Prophet, peace be upon him. > **""The lowest of the people grants protection on behalf of the people.""**"
"9687","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said the protection of Muslims is one; if a woman grants it, do not betray her, for every traitor has a banner on Resurrection Day. > **""The protection of the Muslims is one; if a woman grants protection on their behalf, do not betray her, for every traitor will have a banner on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9688","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger, peace be upon him, granted Abu al-As protection and the Prophet upheld it; and Umm Hani granted her brother Aqil protection and he upheld it. Companion report, prose only."
"9689","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger, peace be upon him, after the migration, sought leave, then announced from the chamber door at dawn prayer that she had granted Abu al-As protection; the Prophet, peace be upon him, said he knew nothing until they heard it, and affirmed the rule. > **""O people, I did not know of this until you heard it; indeed the lowest of the Muslims grants protection on behalf of the Muslims.""**"
"9690","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said the traitor's banner on Resurrection Day is at his back. > **""The banner of the traitor on the Day of Resurrection will be at his back.""**"
"9691","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said every traitor has a banner, Muslim protection is one sought by the lowest, and whoever betrays a Muslim's pledge bears the curse of Allah, angels, and all people, with no deed accepted. > **""Every traitor will have a banner on the Day of Resurrection; the protection of the Muslims is one, the lowest of them strives for it; whoever betrays a Muslim, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; Allah will accept from him neither exchange nor ransom.""**"
"9692","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger, peace be upon him, say the traitor's banner is raised and his deeds named. > **""The traitor will have a banner raised for him, and it will be said: this was for such-and-such, or he did such-and-such.""**"
"9693","Fayruz al-Daylami, may Allah be pleased with him, reported he came to the Prophet, peace be upon him, with the head of al-Aswad al-Ansi. Companion report, prose only."
"9694","Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, reported no head was ever carried to the Messenger, peace be upon him, not even at Badr to Madinah. Companion report, prose only."
"9695","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, passed Abu Qatadah by a man he had killed and ordered him left with his spoils. > **""Leave him and his spoils.""**"
"9696","Al-Shabi reported Jarir challenged Mihran in single combat, killed him, took a girdle worth thirty thousand; men wrote to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who ruled it was not ordinary spoils of arms or mounts, withheld it as extra award, and made it booty. Ruling of Umar, prose only."
"9697","Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah reported at Dabiq under Abu Ubaydah, Habib ibn Maslamah killed the chief of Cyprus with emeralds, rubies, pearls, gold and brocade; Abu Ubaydah wished to take a fifth, Habib cited the killer's right to spoils, and Muadh cited the Prophet, peace be upon him. > **""A man has only what his commander willingly gives him.""**"
"9698","Ibn Sirin reported al-Bara ibn Malik, brother of Anas, killed the chief of Zara, took spoils of thirty thousand; Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, told Abu Talhah they did not take a fifth from spoils, but Bara's gain was great wealth and he saw it should be fif **th**ed. Report of Umar, prose only."
"9699","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them, reported Aqil ibn Abi Talib challenged a man at Mutah, killed him, and the Messenger, peace be upon him, awarded him his ring and spoils. Companion report, prose only."
"9700","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported Abdullah ibn Masud reached Abu Jahl wounded at Badr, beheaded him, took his spoils, reported to the Prophet, peace be upon him, swore thrice by Allah, and was awarded the spoils. Companion report, prose only."
"9701","Khuraym ibn Aws reported none more hostile to Arabs than Hurmuz; after Musaylimah they met him at Kazimah in great force, Khalid ibn al-Walid dueled and killed him, wrote to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, who awarded the spoils; Hurmuz's cap alone was a hundred thousand dirhams, as Persian nobles wore hundred-thousand caps. Historical report, prose only."
"9702","Qays ibn Abi Hazim saw Amr ibn Madikarib at Qadisiyyah urging men to be fierce lions, mocking the Persian spearman; a Persian champion loosed at him, broke his bow-tip, then Amr charged, pierced his spine, dismounted, took two bracelets and a brocade cloak, and it was left to him. Companion report, prose only."
"9703","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said whoever ransoms a captive from the enemy, he himself is that captive in reward. > **""Whoever ransoms a captive from the hands of the enemy, I am that captive.""**"
"9704","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, commanded ransoming Muslim captives and giving their beggars; wealth left is for heirs, debt left is upon him and rulers after him from the Muslim treasury. > **""Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs, and whoever leaves debt, it is upon me and upon the rulers after me from the treasury of the Muslims.""**"
"9705","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, said if bondage were ever affirmed upon any Arab, today there are only captives and ransom. > **""Had bondage been affirmed upon any of the Arabs, today there would be only captives and ransom.""**"
"9706","Abu Rafi reported Umar ibn al-Khattab, leaning on Ibn Abbas with Ibn Amr and Said ibn Zayd present, said he had said nothing on kalalah, appointed no successor, and whoever of Arab captives survived to his death was free from Allah's wealth. Bequest of Umar, prose only."
"9707","Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, forbade snatching a brother's captive to kill him. > **""Let none of you pursue his brother's captive and kill him.""**"
"9708","Alqamah ibn Hilal from his father from his grandfather came with men to the Messenger, peace be upon him, in Madinah after the migration, and found him striking necks of captives at scant water until blood covered the water. Historical report, prose only."
"9709","Abu al-Tufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, smiled and said he saw people driven to Paradise in chains; they were captives whom the Emigrants took and brought into Islam. > **""Why do you not ask me why I smiled?""** > **""I saw people being driven to Paradise in chains.""** > **""A people whom the Emigrants take captive and bring into Islam.""**"
"9710","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, smiled one day and spoke of people driven to Paradise bound in chains. > **""A people driven to Paradise bound together in chains.""**"
"9711","Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, reported at the Trench with the Prophet, peace be upon him, the pick struck rock and he smiled, speaking of people brought from the east in fetters driven to Paradise. > **""I smiled at people brought from the east in fetters, driven to Paradise.""**"
"9712","In his narration: they are driven to Paradise while unwilling. Short report completing the prior, prose with speech. > **""They are driven to Paradise while they are unwilling.""**"
"9713","Abbad ibn Amr reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, sent a detachment, Bedouins were brought, one claimed Islam; asked for witness he named Abbad, who affirmed hearing the testimony, so the Messenger freed them. > **""Who testifies for you?""** > **""O Abbad, did you hear him?""**"
"9714","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, said Allah guiding one polytheist through him is better than red camels. > **""O Muadh, that Allah guide through your hands a man of the polytheists is better for you than red camels.""**"
"9715","Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, told Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that guiding one man is better than all the sun rises and sets upon. > **""That Allah guide through your hands a man is better for you than all upon which the sun has risen and set.""**"
"9716","In a narration the Messenger, peace be upon him, sent Ali to Yemen, bound a banner, then told Abu Rafi to catch him, not call from behind, let him halt unturning until he came, and counseled him likewise. Companion report, prose only."
"9717","Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said whoever guides a man to Islam earns Paradise. > **""Whoever guides a man to Islam through his hands, Paradise becomes due for him.""**"
"9718","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said one who accepts Islam at another's hands is his client. > **""Whoever accepts Islam at the hands of a man, he is his client.""**"
"9719","Adi ibn Hatim, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the horsemen took his aunt and others at Aqrab, lined them before the Prophet, peace be upon him; she pleaded as an aged helpless woman whose provider fled and father was lost; asked her provider, told it was Adi who fled Allah and His Messenger, he freed her and ordered a mount. > **""Who is your provider?""** > **""The one who fled from Allah and His Messenger?""**"
"9720","Abu Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah asked Adi ibn Hatim in Kufah about his report: once hating the Prophet, fleeing to Syria then Rome, his kinswoman seized by Khalid, brought to Madinah, pleading three days for freedom as the fugitive's kin, until freed, lodged, and carried by a Tayy caravan, urging Adi to come before others. Historical report, prose only."
"9721","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the Messenger, peace be upon him, said whatever one accepts Islam upon belongs to him. > **""Whoever accepts Islam upon a thing, it belongs to him.""**"
"9722","Razin ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, fearing neighbors would seize his well after Islam appeared, asked the Prophet, peace be upon him, who wrote: from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah, they keep their well and home if truthful; Madinah judges ever ruled by it, with kan written kawn. Companion report, prose only."
"9723","Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, said at Jiranah on Khaybar day ten are lawful for Muslims on campaigns: honey, water, raisins, vinegar, salt, earth, stone, unwrought wood, fresh skin, and food brought forth. > **""Ten are lawful for the Muslims on their campaigns: honey, water, raisins, vinegar, salt, earth, stone, wood not yet carved, fresh skin, and food brought forth thereby.""**"
"9724","Khalid ibn Umayr reported they campaigned with Utbah ibn Ghazwan, opened Ubullah, found a ship of walnuts, thought them the smoothest stones, cracked and ate them as rich food. Campaign report, prose only."
"9725","From Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Some people wish to draw me away from my religion, but that will never be until I meet Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his Companions. He also reported that whoever sells food or fodder obtained in the land of Rome for gold or silver, the fifth becomes due upon it, the fifth of Allah and the share of the Muslims."
"9726","From Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, slaughtered a camel, and the people rushed to plunder it. His caller then proclaimed that Allah and His Messenger forbid you from plundering. The people then brought back what they had taken, and he divided it among them. > **""Allah and His Messenger forbid you from plundering.""**"
"9727","From a man of Banu Layth, who said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, captured me, and I remained with them. They came upon some sheep, seized them by plunder, and began cooking them. He said: I then heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""Plunder is not right, so overturn the pots.""**"
"9728","From Abu Layla, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I witnessed the conquest of Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. When they were defeated, we fell upon their dwellings. The people took whatever sheep they found and the pots were soon boiling, but they were overturned. He then divided among us, assigning one sheep for every ten men."
"9729","From Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to forbid plunder, to the extent that he would order a company cooking the meat of a sheep not to eat it. > **""Do not eat it.""**"
"9730","From Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade plunder and said: > **""Whoever plunders is not from us.""**"
"9731","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The people seized sheep by plunder, slaughtered them, and began cooking them. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then came and ordered the pots to be overturned, and said: > **""Plunder is not lawful.""**"
"9732","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""He is not from us who plunders, or despoils, or points toward despoiling.""**"
"9733","From Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Plunder is not lawful.""**"
"9734","From al-Irbad ibn Sariyah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would take a hair from the fay of Allah and say: > **""I have no share in this beyond that of any one of you, except the fifth, and the fifth is returned among you. So return the thread and the needle and what is above that, and beware of misappropriation, for it is disgrace and Fire and shame upon its doer on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9735","From Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: They said, O Messenger of Allah, your freedman so-and-so has been martyred. He said: > **""No. I have seen upon him a cloak which he misappropriated on such-and-such a day.""**"
"9736","From Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from one who heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he was in Wadi al-Qura upon a horse, that a man came and said: Your freedman, or your boy, so-and-so has been martyred. He said: > **""Rather he is being dragged to the Fire on account of a cloak which he misappropriated.""**"
"9737","From al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib al-Kindi, may Allah be pleased with him, that he sat with Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah have mercy on him, and Abu al-Darda or al-Harith ibn Muawiyah al-Kindi, may Allah be pleased with them, and they recalled the hadith of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Abu al-Darda said to Ubadah, O Ubadah, what were the words of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in a campaign concerning the fifths? Ubadah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, led them in prayer in a campaign beside a camel from the division. When he gave the salutation, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood, took a hair between his fingertips, and said: > **""This is from your spoils, and I have no share in it beyond my share with you except the fifth, and the fifth is returned to you. So return the thread and the needle, and what is greater and what is smaller, and do not misappropriate, for misappropriation is Fire and disgrace upon its people in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"9738","From Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, that he informed Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, when he asked him about the man who asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for a rope before division. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Leave it until it is divided, then if you wish we shall give you a rope, and if you wish we shall give you repeatedly.""**"
"9739","From Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until we reached Baqi al-Gharqad. He turned to me and said: > **""Do you hear what I hear?""** I said, may my father and mother be ransom for you, no, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""This is so-and-so son of so-and-so, being punished in his grave on account of a shawl which he misappropriated on the day of Khaybar.""**"
"9740","From Habib ibn Maslamah, who said: I heard Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: > **""If my nation does not misappropriate, no enemy will ever stand against them.""** Abu Dharr said to Habib ibn Maslamah, did the enemy remain with you for the time of milking a sheep? He said, yes, and for the milking of three rich sheep. Abu Dharr said, you have misappropriated, by the Lord of the Kabah."
"9741","From Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came until he was at al-Jiranah. The people crowded upon him and his cloak caught upon a tree. He said: > **""Return my cloak to me. Do you fear that I will not divide among you? Were there as many cattle as the trees of Tihamah, I would divide them among you, then you would not find me cowardly nor miserly nor lying.""** Then he said: > **""Return the thread and the needle, for misappropriation is disgrace and Fire and shame upon its people on the Day of Resurrection.""** And he said, taking a hair from the hump of a camel: > **""I have nothing from the fay beyond this hair, except the fifth, and the fifth is returned to you.""**"
"9742","From Abu Hazim al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A leather mat from the spoils was brought to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and it was said, take shade with it, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""Would you love to be shaded among yourselves with shade of Fire on the Day of Resurrection?""**"
"9743","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""A believer does not misappropriate.""**"
"9744","From Amr ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""There is no secret theft and no misappropriation, and whoever misappropriates will bring what he misappropriated on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"9745","From Kharijah ibn Amr, who was an ally of Abu Sufyan in the days of ignorance, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said, taking a hair from the hump of his she-camel: > **""O people, it is not lawful for me nor for anyone from the spoils of the Muslims even what weighs this hair, beyond what Allah has prescribed for me.""**"
"9746","From al-Mistawrid al-Fihri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Return the thread and the needle. Whoever misappropriates a needle or a thread will be charged on the Day of Resurrection to bring it, and he will not be able to bring it.""**"
"9747","From Abu Burdah ibn Niyar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, visited the tribes supplicating for them, but left one tribe he did not visit. They disapproved of that, searched the goods of a companion of theirs, and found a necklace in the saddle of one of their men which he had misappropriated. They returned it, so he came to them and prayed for them."
"9748","From Rabiah al-Jurashi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Whoever conceals misappropriated spoils is like the one who took them.""**"
"9749","From Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I saw the spoils divided into five parts, then lots were cast upon them, and what fell to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was his to choose from."
"9750","From Abu al-Zubayr, who said: Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, was asked, how did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, dispose of the fifth? He said: He would mount a man with it in the path of Allah, then another man, then another man."
"9751","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent an expedition and they gained spoils, he would divide the spoils into fifths, striking it into five, then recite, Know that whatever you gain, a fifth is for Allah, until His saying concerning what is in the heavens and the earth. He made the share of Allah and the share of the Messenger one, and the share of the near kin, making these two shares strength in horses and weapons. He made the share of the orphans, the needy, and the wayfarer for none besides them. He made the remaining four shares: two shares for the horse, one share for its rider, and one share for the foot soldier."
"9752","From Tariq ibn Shihab, that the people of Basrah campaigned to Nihawand and the people of Kufah reinforced them under Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, and they prevailed. The people of Basrah wished not to share with the people of Kufah. A man of Banu Tamim or Banu Utarid said, O slave with the severed ear, do you wish to share in our spoils, though his ear had been cut while with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said, you have reviled my best ear. He wrote to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and Umar wrote: Spoils belong to whoever witnessed the encounter."
"9753","From al-Qasim, who said: Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By the One besides whom there is no deity, Allah apportioned this fay upon the tongue of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, before Persia and Rome were conquered."
"9754","From Abu Malik al-Ashari, may Allah be pleased with him, that he and his companions came in a ship, with a piebald horse with him. When they landed they found many camels of the polytheists and took them. Abu Malik ordered them to slaughter a camel and take aid from it. Then he went on foot until he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and informed him of his journey, his companions, and the camels they had taken. Then he returned to his companions. Those with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said, give us, O Messenger of Allah, from these camels. He said: > **""Go to Abu Malik.""** When they came to him, he divided them into fifths, sending a fifth to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, taking a third of what remained after the fifth and dividing it among his companions, and the remaining two thirds for the Muslims, dividing it among them. They came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said, we have never seen the like of Abu Malik with these spoils. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Had I been there, I would have done nothing but as he did.""**"
"9755","From Jubayr ibn Mutim, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not allot to Banu Abd Shams nor to Banu Nawfal from the fifth anything, as he allotted to Banu Hashim and Banu al-Muttalib. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, would divide the fifth like the division of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, except that he did not give the kin of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave them. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, would give them, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, after him."
"9756","From Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When fay came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he would divide it the same day, giving the settled folk two shares and the bedouins one share. We were called, and I was called before Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, and given one share, and he was displeased until that was seen in his face by the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and those present. There remained some gold, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kept lifting it with the tip of his staff and it would fall, then he would lift it and it would fall, saying: > **""How will you be on the day treasure is hoarded for you from this?""** None answered him. Ammar ibn Yasir said, by Allah, we would wish it were hoarded for us, so let him who is patient be patient and him who is tried be tried. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: > **""Perhaps you will be among them most sorely tried.""**"
"9757","From Abu Layla, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided sheep, assigning to every ten of his Companions a sheep."
"9758","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allotted at Hunayn for eighty horses, two shares each."
"9759","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave the slow mule nothing, and gave it less in strength and quality than the share of the Arabian horse. Al-kawdan is the slow mule."
"9760","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave on the day of Badr to the horse two shares and to the man one share."
"9761","From al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave al-Zubayr one share, his mother one share, and his horse two shares."
"9762","From Abu Ruhm and his brother, may Allah be pleased with them, that they were two horsemen on the day of Hunayn, and were given six shares, four for their two horses and two for themselves, and they sold the two shares for two young camels."
"9763","From al-Miqdad ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, that on the day of Badr he was upon a horse called Subhah, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allotted for him one share for his horse and one share for himself."
"9764","From Abu Kabshah al-Anmari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, conquered Mecca, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, was over the left flank and al-Miqdad, may Allah be pleased with him, was over the right flank. When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered Mecca and the people settled, they brought their two horses. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood and wiped the dust from their faces with his garment and said: > **""I have assigned two shares for the horse and one share for the horseman, so whoever violates it, Allah will requite him.""**"
"9765","From Abu Ruhm, may Allah be pleased with him, from his brother, that they were two horsemen on the day of Khaybar and were given six shares, four for their two horses and two for themselves, and they sold the two shares for two young camels."
"9766","From Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allotted two shares for the horse and one share for the man."
"9767","From Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: > **""Whoever finds his property in the fay before it is divided, he is most entitled to it, and whoever finds it after it is divided, he has nothing.""**"
"9768","From Sufyan ibn Wahb al-Khawlani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When we conquered Egypt without treaty, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and said, O Amr ibn al-As, divide it. Amr said, I shall not divide it. Al-Zubayr said, by Allah, you shall divide it as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided Khaybar. Amr said, by Allah, I shall not divide it until I write to the Commander of the Faithful. He wrote to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and Umar wrote to him: Leave it standing until posterity may campaign from it."
"9769","From Aslam, freedman of Umar, who said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: If I live till this coming year, no town will be opened for the people but I shall divide it among them as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided Khaybar."
"9770","From Qabisah ibn Jabir, from his father, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him: I wish to divide the Sawad of Kufah among the Muslims who prevailed. Sad wrote to him: O Commander of the Faithful, we have prevailed over the most tender-hearted of the people Allah created, the most generous in spirit, the greatest in blessing, the most open-handed. Their hands are for food and their tongues for peace. If you see fit, O Commander of the Faithful, do not separate them, nor divide them, nor turn us from the course in which Allah has granted us what He has granted. For the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to say that the might of the Arabs lies in the points of their spears and the hooves of their horses."
"9771","From Nashir ibn Sumayy al-Yazani, who said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of al-Jabiyah, addressing the people: Allah, mighty and majestic, has made me treasurer and distributor of this wealth, rather Allah distributes it, and I begin with the household of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then the noblest among them. He fixed for the wives of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ten thousand each, except Juwayriyah, Safiyyah, and Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with them. Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was just among us, so Umar dealt justly among them. Then he said: I begin with my companions the first Emigrants, for we were expelled from our homes by wrong and enmity, then the noblest among them. He fixed for the people of Badr among them five thousand, for those of the Ansar who witnessed Badr four thousand, and for those who witnessed Uhud three thousand. He said: Whoever hastened in emigration, his grant is hastened, and whoever delayed in emigration, his grant is delayed, so let no man blame but the kneeling place of his mount. I apologize to you for dismissing Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him. I ordered him to reserve this wealth for the weak among the Emigrants, but he gave it to the strong, the noble, and the eloquent, so I removed him and appointed Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Amr ibn Hafs said: By Allah, you have offered no excuse, O Umar ibn al-Khattab. You have removed an agent employed by the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sheathed a sword drawn by the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, lowered a banner raised by the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, severed kinship, and envied your cousin. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You are near of kin, young in age, partisan for your cousin."
"9772","From Umar ibn Abdullah, freedman of Ghifarah, who said: Wealth came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, from Bahrain, and he said: Whoever has a promise from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, let him come and take. Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, came and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, promised me, saying, when wealth comes to me from Bahrain, I shall give you thus and thus and thus, filling his two hands three times. He said: Take with your hands. He took with his hands and found five hundred, was told to return, then was given the like of it. Then the rest was divided among the people, ten dirhams to each. The next year more wealth came, and each received twenty dirhams, with surplus remaining. He said to the people: O people, surplus remains of this wealth, and you have servants who serve you and work for you. If you wish, we shall grant them something. He granted them five dirhams each. They said, O Caliph of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would that you prefer the Emigrants. He said: Their reward is upon Allah. These are livelihoods; equality therein is better than preference. When Abu Bakr died and Umar succeeded, may Allah be pleased with them, Allah granted him conquests and greater wealth came. He said: Abu Bakr had one view concerning this wealth, and I have another. I shall not make one who fought the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, like one who fought with him. He preferred the Emigrants and the Helpers, fixing five thousand for those who witnessed Badr among them, four thousand for those whose Islam preceded that of the people of Badr, twelve thousand for each wife of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, except Safiyyah and Juwayriyah, may Allah be pleased with them, for whom he fixed six thousand each, but they refused to take it, saying it was fixed for their place with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, not for emigration, and theirs was like the place of the others, so he saw the justice of that and made them equal. He fixed for al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, twelve thousand for kinship to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with them both, four thousand, and for al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, five thousand each, joining them to their father for their kinship to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He fixed for Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, three thousand, who said, O father, you fixed for Usamah ibn Zayd more, though neither his father had merit over you nor he over me. He said: His father was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, than your father, and he was more beloved to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, than you. He fixed for the sons of the Emigrants who witnessed Badr two thousand each. Umar ibn Abi Salamah passed, and he said, add a thousand for him, boy, for his father Abu Salamah two thousand, and I add a thousand for his mother Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her. If you had a mother like Umm Salamah, I would add a thousand for you. He fixed for Uthman ibn Abdullah, nephew of Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, may Allah be pleased with him, eight hundred, and for al-Nadr ibn Anas two thousand dirhams. Talhah said, the son of Uthman like him came and you fixed eight hundred, while a youth of the Ansar came and you fixed two thousand. He said: I met the father of this one on the day of Uhud asking after the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I said, I see him slain. He drew his sword, set his wrist, and said, if the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is slain, Allah is Living and does not die, and fought until slain. Shall he who herds sheep be made like him? Umar acted upon this all his life, until the year he performed pilgrimage, when some said, when the Commander of the Faithful dies we shall appoint so-and-so, meaning Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, and that the pledge to Abu Bakr was sudden. He wished to speak in the days of tashriq at Mina, but Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, said, O Commander of the Faithful, this gathering is dominated by the rabble who will not retain it. Wait until we come to the land of emigration, the abode of faith, with your companions among the Emigrants and Helpers, and speak your speech, and your speech will be retained. He hastened until he came to Medina, went out on Friday, praised Allah and said: Word has reached me of one who says, when Umar dies we shall appoint so-and-so. Yes, by Allah, the pledge to Abu Bakr was sudden, but where is the like of Abu Bakr for us to stretch our necks to him as we stretched them to Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr saw fit to divide equally, while I saw fit to prefer. If I live to this year, I shall return to the view of Abu Bakr, for his view is better than mine. I have seen a dream, and I see my end has drawn near. I saw as though a red cock pecked me three pecks. I asked Asma, and she said, a foreign slave will kill you. If I perish, your affair is to these six with whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died pleased: Uthman ibn Affan, Ali ibn Abi Talib, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Talhah ibn Abdullah, and Sad ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them. If I live, I shall make a covenant by which you will not perish. Stoning is a truth; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stoned and we stoned after him. Were it not that they would say Umar wrote what is not in the Book of Allah, I would have written it. He recited from the Book of Allah: The aged man and woman, if they commit adultery, stone them outright as recompense from Allah, and Allah is Mighty, Wise. I considered the paternal aunt and the brother's daughter, and did not make them heirs. If I live, I shall open for you a way concerning them you will know. If I perish, Allah is my successor over you, and you shall choose your view. I have compiled the register and founded the garrison towns. I fear for you only two men: one who interprets the Quran otherwise than its interpretation and fights over it, and one who deems himself more deserving of rule than his fellow and fights over it. He spoke this speech on Friday and died on Wednesday."
"9773","From Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave us a share from Khaybar, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, also gave it to us. When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came and the people crowded upon him, he sent to us and said: The people have crowded upon me. If you wish, I shall give you wealth in place of your share from Khaybar. We looked at one another and said, yes. Then Umar was stabbed before giving us anything, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, took it and refused to give us, saying: Umar had taken it from you."
"9774","From Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that a small chest came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. His companions looked at it but did not know its value. He said: Will you permit me to send it to Aisha, for the love of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for her? They said, yes. It was brought to Aisha, and she opened it. It was said, Umar ibn al-Khattab has sent this to you. She said: What was opened upon Ibn al-Khattab after the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? O Allah, do not keep me alive for his gift next year."
"9775","From Makhlad al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him, that three slaves witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to grant each of them a thousand."
"9776","From Musab ibn Sad, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, fixed a thousand each for the Emigrant women, among them Umm Abdullah."
"9777","From Nafi, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz would not fix for any who had not reached puberty but a hundred dirhams, and would not fix for a newborn until weaned. While circling one night at the prayer place, he heard an infant crying and said to its mother, nurse him. She said, the Commander of the Faithful does not fix for a newborn until weaned, and I have weaned him. Umar said, I have nearly killed him. Nurse him, for the Commander of the Faithful will fix for him. Thereafter he fixed for him, and for the newborn from birth."
"9778","From Thabit ibn al-Harith al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, allotted on the day of Khaybar for Sahlah bint Asim and for a daughter born to her."
"9779","From Zaynab, wife of Abdullah al-Thaqafi, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave her at Khaybar fifty wasqs of dates, and at Medina twenty wasqs of barley."
"9780","From Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he would grant additional spoils in his campaigns."
"9781","From al-Saib ibn Yazid, from his father, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted us an additional gift besides a share of the fifth, and I received an aged she-camel."
"9782","From Man ibn Yazid, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: No spoils are lawful until divided, and no additional gift until divided for the people."
"9783","From Muadh, meaning Ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent me to Arab villages and ordered me to take the share of the land. Sufyan said: The share of the land is a third or a quarter."
"9784","From Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said, O Messenger of Allah, write for me for such-and-such land in Sham, over which the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had not yet prevailed. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Do you hear what this man says?""** Abu Thalabah said, by the One in whose Hand is my soul, it will surely be conquered. He said: So he wrote it for me. Then he mentioned the hadith."
"9785","From Tamim al-Dari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I sought from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, land in Sham before it was conquered, and he gave it to me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, conquered it in his time. I came to him and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave me land from such to such. Umar made a third of it for the wayfarer, a third for its caretakers, and a third for us."
"9786","From Amr ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted to Bilal ibn al-Harith al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, the mines of al-Qabaliyyah, its highlands and its lowlands, and where cultivation is possible of Quds."
"9787","From Bilal ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted him this estate and wrote for him: In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave to Bilal ibn al-Harith. He gave him the mines of al-Qabaliyyah, its lowlands and highlands, its herbage and Dhat al-Nusub, and where cultivation is possible of Quds, if he is truthful. Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote it."
"9788","From Bilal ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted him all of al-Aqiq."
"9789","From Abu Hind al-Dari, may Allah be pleased with him, that they came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, six men: Aws ibn Kharijah ibn Sawadan ibn Judhaymah ibn Dharra ibn Adi ibn al-Dar, his brother Tamim ibn Aws, Yazid ibn Qays, Abu Hind ibn Abdullah, his brother al-Tayyib ibn Abdullah, whom the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, named Abd al-Rahman, and Fakihah ibn al-Numan. They accepted Islam and asked him to give them land from the land of Sham. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Ask where you wish.""** They rose from him consulting about a place to ask of him. Tamim said, I think we should ask for Bayt al-Maqdis and its district. Abu Hind said, do you see the king of the Persians today, is he not in Bayt al-Maqdis? Tamim said, yes."
"9790","From Husayn ibn Mushammit, may Allah be pleased with him, that he came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, pledged the pledge of Islam, rendered the charity of his wealth, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted him several waters at al-Marwath, from the tract of Hirad, among them Usayhib, among them al-Maizah, among them Ahwad, among them al-Mihad, and among them al-Sadirah. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stipulated upon Husayn ibn Mushammit concerning what he granted him that he not destroy its pasture, nor sell its water, nor withhold its surplus. Zuhayr ibn Asim ibn Husayn composed verse: My lands were not barren, wherein is the line of the noble pen, from the Prophet when he gave the people, leaving no confusion nor obscurity."
"9791","From Awfa ibn Mawalah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he granted me al-Amim, stipulating upon me the wayfarer first to drink. He granted Saidah, a man of us, a well in the desert called al-Jaubiyyah, a well in which wealth was hidden, not of sweet water. He granted some people Muadah al-Ura, below al-Yamamah. We had all come to him, and he wrote for each man of us to that effect on leather."
"9792","From Ruzayn ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When Islam prevailed, we had a well at al-Daninah, and we feared those around us would overpower us for it. He said: I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that to him. He said: So he wrote for us a document: From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, as to what follows: They shall have their well, if he is truthful. He said: We never litigated concerning it before any judge of Medina but they judged for us by it, for the document of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was present, and he claimed it was the document of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah. As to what follows: They shall have their well, if he is truthful.""**"
"9793","From Abu al-Saib, from his grandmother, who was among the emigrant women, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, granted her a well at al-Aqiq."
"9794","From Utayr al-Udhri, may Allah be pleased with him, that he sought from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, land in Wadi al-Qura, which today is called Buwayrat Utayr. He said: I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he halted at Tabuk, pray in Wadi al-Qura."
"9795","From Mujjaah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave Mujjaah ibn Murarah of Banu Sulma land in al-Yamamah called al-Awzah. He said: He wrote for him a document: From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to Mujjaah ibn Murarah of Banu Sulma: I have given you al-Awzah, so whoever opposes me concerning it, the Fire. Yazid wrote it. > **""From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Mujjaah ibn Murarah of Banu Sulma: I have given you al-Awzah, so whoever opposes me concerning it, the Fire.""**"
"9796","From Qaylah bint Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she was under Habib ibn Azhar, brother of Banu Khabbab, and bore him daughters. Then he died, and Ayyub ibn Azhar, their uncle, took her daughters from her. She set out seeking companionship to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in early Islam. Juwayriyah among them, Hudayba, wept, seized by trembling, the youngest, wearing a woolen garment. She carried her with her. While they urged the camel, a hare started up, and the discerning Hudayba said: No, by Allah, your ankle shall ever remain above the ankle of Ayyub in this affair. Then a fox appeared, which she named by other names, forgotten by Abdullah ibn Hassan, and said concerning it what she said concerning the hare. While they journeyed, the camel knelt and trembled, and the discerning Hudayba said: I have attained, by Allah, what seized Ayyub. Qaylah said: It trembled toward her. Woe to you, what shall I do? She said: Turn your garments inside out, roll your back to your belly, and turn the saddle cloths of your camel. She removed her garment, turned it, and rolled her back to her belly. When I did as she ordered, the camel shook, stood, and opened its legs. Hudayba said: Restore your gear. I did as she ordered and restored it. Then we journeyed, and Ayyub came hastening after us with drawn sword. We took refuge at a large settlement, until the camel reached the middle house, a tractable camel. I rushed inside with the girl, and he reached me with the sword, its edge striking part of my head. He said, hand over my brother's daughter, O foul one. I cast her to him, he placed her upon his shoulder and went. I knew him better than the household, and went to a sister of mine married among Banu Shayban, seeking companionship to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in early Islam. While with her one night, she supposing my eyes asleep, her husband came from Sham and said, by your father, I have found for Qaylah a truthful companion. She said, who is he? He said, Hurayth ibn Hassan al-Shaybani, delegate of Bakr ibn Wail to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, this morning. My sister said, woe to me, let not my sister hear of this, lest she go out with the brother of Bakr ibn Wail between ear and eye of the earth with no man of her people. He said, do not mention it to her, for I shall not mention it. She heard what they said. In the morning she saddled her camel, found him not far, and asked his companionship. He said, yes, with honor, his mounts kneeling. She went with him a truthful companion until they came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while he prayed the dawn prayer with the people. It had been established at daybreak, the stars latticed in the sky, men hardly known in the darkness. She stood with the men, a woman fresh from ignorance. The man beside her in the row said, are you woman or man? She said, woman. He said, you nearly tempted me, pray in the women's row behind you. A row of women had newly formed by the chambers, unseen when she entered. She remained therein until sunrise drew near. She saw a man of presence and cheer, her sight aspiring to see the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, above the people, until a man came after sunrise and said, peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah.""** He wore two worn garments once dyed with saffron, now faded, with a stripped palm stalk in his hand lacking all but two fronds above, sitting squatting. When she saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, humble in sitting, she trembled from fear. His companion said, O Messenger of Allah, the poor woman trembles. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to her, without looking at her while she was behind his back: > **""O poor woman, calmness be upon you.""** When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said it, Allah removed what had entered her heart of terror. Her companion, Hurayth ibn Hassan, first man, advanced and pledged Islam for himself and his people, then said, O Messenger of Allah, write between us and Banu Tamim for al-Dahna, none of them to cross to us but a traveler or a neighbor. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Write for him al-Dahna, boy.""** When she saw him inclining to him, though it was her homeland and abode, she said, O Messenger of Allah, he has not pursued the right course. This al-Dahna is at the camel's rest and the sheep's pasture, with the women and sons of Banu Tamim beyond it. He said: > **""Hold, boy. The poor woman has spoken truth. The Muslim is brother of the Muslim. Water and trees suffice them both, and they aid one another against the tempter.""** When Hurayth saw his document withheld, he struck one hand upon the other and said, I and you are as it is said: A ewe carries its death in its hooves. She said, by Allah, I knew you, if a guide in darkness, steady at the saddle, chaste with a female companion, until we came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, but blame me not for asking my share when you asked yours. He said, what is your share in al-Dahna? She said, the resting of my camel you ask for your wife's camel. He said, without doubt, I call the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to witness that I am to you brother and companion while I live, if you uphold this with him. She said, since I began it, I shall not neglect it. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""Is the son of this woman blamed for clarifying the plan and aiding from behind the chamber?""** She wept and said, yes, by Allah, I bore him, O Messenger of Allah, unlawfully conceived, then he fought with you on the day of al-Rabdhah, then went with my provisions from Khaybar, seized by its fever, and died, leaving women upon me. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: > **""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, were you not poor, you would have been dragged upon your face. Can not one of you keep good company with her companion in this world in kindness? When there intervenes between him and her One more entitled to him than her, let her say: My Lord, recompense me for what has passed, and aid me in what remains. By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, one of you weeps and her companion weeps for her. O servants of Allah, do not torment your dead.""** Then he wrote for her on a piece of red leather: For Qaylah and the women among the daughters of Qaylah, their right shall not be wronged, nor forced upon an undesired marriage, and every believer and Muslim is a helper to them. Do good and do not do evil."
"9797","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Magian left the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, I asked him, and he informed me that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave him choice between the jizya and death, and he chose the jizya."
"9798","From Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Magians had a book they recited and knowledge they studied. Their leader committed adultery, and they wished to apply the penalty upon him. He said to them, did not Adam marry his sons to his daughters? They did not apply the penalty, and the book was lifted. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took the jizya, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and I."
"9799","From al-Saib ibn Yazid, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took the jizya from the Magians of Hajar, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, took it from the Magians of Persia, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, took it from the Berbers."
"9800","On the authority of Muslim ibn al-Ala al-Hadrami, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when he gave counsel to al-Ala at the time he directed him to Bahrain. He instructed him concerning the obligations and the established practices, and he wrote for al-Ala instructing that the Magians be treated according to the practice applied to the People of the Book. > **""It is not lawful for one who is ignorant of the obligatory duties and the Sunan, but lawful for him is what is besides that.""** > **""Apply to the Magians the practice of the People of the Book.""**"
"9801","On the authority of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: > **""Whoever accepts Islam, there is no jizya upon him.""**"
"9802","On the authority of Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would not fight on behalf of any of the polytheists except on behalf of the people under covenant of protection. > **""He would not fight on behalf of any of the polytheists except on behalf of the dhimmis.""**"
"9803","From Ghurfah ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with him, who was a Companion and who fought with Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl, may Allah be pleased with him, in Yemen during the apostasy wars, that he passed by a Christian from the people of Egypt called al-Mandiqun and invited him to Islam. The Christian then mentioned the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and spoke abusively of him. The matter was reported to Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, who sent for Ghurfah. Amr said, we have granted them a covenant. Ghurfah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah forbid that we should have granted them covenants and pledges that they may harm us concerning Allah and His Messenger. We granted them covenant only that we leave them with their churches to say therein what they wish, that we not burden them beyond their capacity, that we fight in their defense, and that we leave them to their own laws unless they come to us, in which case we judge between them by what Allah has revealed. Upon that Amr said: You have spoken the truth."
"9804","From Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he saw a Christian driving a woman along, then he struck at her so that she fell, and he violated her. Awf struck him with a piece of wood he had with him and wounded his head. Then Awf went to Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Grant me protection from Amr, for I feared his haste. Muadh went to Amr and informed him, and Amr gathered them together. He kept questioning the Christian until he confessed. Amr then ordered a piece of wood to be shaped, and said: These people have a covenant, so fulfill the covenant toward them so long as they fulfill it toward you, but if they alter it then they have no covenant. Then by his order the man was crucified."
